          "text": "Use drillthrough filters to save chart visibility states on the same page."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a field parameter as storage for every chart’s visibility and filter settings."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use scheduled refresh to alternate between overview and exception report layouts."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-013",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-013",
      "topic": "Page navigation versus drillthrough",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "prompt": "A Help button should open a static instructions page, with no selected business entity or filter context. Which button action is most appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use Back to return to whichever report page the user opened previously."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use CustomerID drillthrough to require a selected customer before opening Help."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Navigate to a bookmark containing customer-analysis state on the current page."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use Page navigation with Help as its destination."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-014",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-014",
      "topic": "Missing drillthrough menu",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "prompt": "A report viewer right-clicks a segment in a chart but no configured drillthrough page appears. The target drillthrough well contains CustomerID, while the source visual only contains ProductCategory. What should the author investigate first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Enable cross-report drillthrough even though both pages are in the same report."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Grant Build permission while leaving the source visual’s fields unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use ProductCategory instead and assume it uniquely identifies the selected customer."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Include the target CustomerID drillthrough field in the source visual context."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-015",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-015",
      "topic": "Cross-filter default pitfalls",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "prompt": "A user says drilling from Year to Month in a hierarchy should also update every other chart on the page. Drill mode currently affects only the selected chart. What feature can the author consider?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Enable drill filters for the page and review the receiving visual interactions."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Enable cross-highlighting from the receiving visuals back to the hierarchy chart."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Synchronize a date slicer without changing the hierarchy chart’s drill settings."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Set every other chart to a permanent filter for the latest available month."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-016",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-016",
      "topic": "RLS and workspace rights",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A sales analyst must see only her own region under a configured RLS role and must not edit workspace content. Which access is most suitable?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Give Viewer access and assign the configured regional RLS role."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Give Member access and assign the same RLS role to the analyst."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Give Contributor access and apply a report filter for the analyst’s region."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Grant Build and omit RLS assignment because Build supplies regional restrictions."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-017",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-017",
      "topic": "Remove or change workspace roles",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A workspace Member attempts to remove a Contributor’s workspace access and cannot. What is the documented administrative remedy?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Give semantic-model Read permission to the workspace Member attempting removal."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Have the Member republish the report without the Contributor’s name in its metadata."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Ask a workspace Admin to remove the Contributor’s workspace access."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Have the Contributor stop sharing reports while retaining the workspace role."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-018",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-018",
      "topic": "App audiences vs editing",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "HR and Finance groups need different report sets through one app, without workspace editing rights or other direct report access. Which delivery design best supports this?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Give both groups Contributor and hide the unwanted reports in workspace navigation."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Make both groups workspace Members and assign different default landing pages."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use app audiences with per-group content access and no workspace editing roles."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Send each group editable PBIX copies and treat later app permission changes as revocation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-019",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-019",
      "topic": "Build versus read",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "An analyst can view an existing shared report but cannot build a new report from its semantic model. Which additional permission may be needed, subject to licensing?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Power BI report-level bookmark access."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Only the ability to receive a report subscription."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Changing their slicer settings to show all regions."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Build permission on the semantic model."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-020",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-020",
      "topic": "Build is not a data firewall",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "Sensitive salary fields are hidden from the report canvas. A model user has Read access. Can removing Build permission alone guarantee that the salaries cannot be discovered through all means?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Yes; Read without Build is an absolute data security boundary."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "No, because Build automatically assigns workspace Admin to every user."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Yes; simply hiding salary columns in Model view encrypts them."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "No; secure sensitive data using appropriate model/data permissions and OLS/RLS rather than relying solely on Build removal."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-021",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-021",
      "topic": "Workspace role effective privileges",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A user belongs to a security group assigned Viewer and another group assigned Contributor for the same workspace. What workspace capability should an admin expect?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The user receives Admin access automatically when roles conflict."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Conflicting roles cancel all workspace access."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "The user receives the highest role permissions from group membership, including Contributor capabilities."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Viewer always overrides every higher role."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-022",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-022",
      "topic": "Semantic model Write permission",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A service account needs to republish or modify a semantic model through supported endpoints but should not automatically become a workspace Admin. What semantic-model permission governs write operations?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Build alone, because creating a dependent report updates the source model."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Read alone, because reading implies republishing."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Write permission, with other licensing or endpoint constraints considered separately."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Reshare alone, because adding users enables model editing."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-023",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-023",
      "topic": "Analyze in Excel requirement",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A Viewer can consume a report, but needs to perform Analyze in Excel on its shared semantic model. Which targeted permission should the model owner review?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Workspace Admin access as the mandatory minimum."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Build permission on the semantic model, subject to the applicable license/capacity constraints."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change report page orientation to landscape."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change a local bookmark to include the raw fact table."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-024",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-024",
      "topic": "Fact-to-dimension design",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "prompt": "An order-line fact table has 300,000 rows with repeated CustomerID values. A Customer table has exactly one row per CustomerID. Which relationship is the normal star-schema starting point?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "A bidirectional relationship between all facts and dimensions regardless of filter ambiguity."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Customer on the one side filtering OrderLine on the many side."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "OrderLine on the one side and Customer on the many side, because orders have more rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "A many-to-many relationship to every dimension by default."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-025",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-025",
      "topic": "Filter preservation with KEEPFILTERS",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "prompt": "A page already filters FactSales[Channel] to Online. A measure must additionally restrict Channel to Partner while preserving, rather than replacing, the page restriction. Which DAX filter modifier produces the intersection?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "ALL(FactSales[Channel]) used to clear all channel restrictions."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "REMOVEFILTERS(FactSales[Channel]) before aggregating."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Sales], FactSales[Channel] = \"Partner\") without KEEPFILTERS."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "KEEPFILTERS(FactSales[Channel] = \"Partner\") inside CALCULATE."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-026",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-026",
      "topic": "Distinct count BLANK semantics",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "prompt": "A CustomerKey column contains 10, 10, BLANK, 20. The analyst wants to count all distinct values, treating BLANK as a distinct value. Which expression fits?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "DISTINCTCOUNT(Fact[CustomerKey]), returning 3 for this fixture."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "COUNTROWS(Fact), returning 3 for this fixture."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK(Fact[CustomerKey]), returning 3 for this fixture."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "COUNT(Fact[CustomerKey]), returning 3 for this fixture."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V57-027",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V57-027",
      "topic": "Replace rather than intersect context",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "prompt": "A page filters Sales[Channel] to Store. A KPI must show Online sales for every page selection of Channel, while preserving other unrelated filters. What is the simplest relevant DAX pattern?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Total Sales], Sales[Channel] = \"Online\") to overwrite the filter on Channel."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "SUMX(ALL(Sales), [Total Sales]) because iterators always respect Channel slicers."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Total Sales], REMOVEFILTERS(Sales)) to ignore all sales filters."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Total Sales], KEEPFILTERS(Sales[Channel] = \"Online\")) to intersect Store and Online."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V61-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V61-001",
      "topic": "Import model refresh versus report automatic page refresh",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Configure automatic page refresh",
      "prompt": "An operations wallboard uses a Power BI Import semantic model that refreshes from a database every 30 minutes. The report author turns on report page-level settings expecting every open viewer to query the database every 30 seconds. Which change actually enables automatic page refresh at a fixed interval, subject to service capacity constraints?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep Import storage and turn on a visual tooltip to query the database at every hover."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a supported DirectQuery connection for the monitored data, configure fixed-interval page refresh, and check service/capacity minimum intervals."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Keep Import and enable scheduled semantic model refresh; this makes an open report query the source on each page refresh tick."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Enable a personal bookmark for every viewer so that the underlying source updates each time the bookmark opens."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V61-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V61-002",
      "topic": "Enterprise gateway for private-network semantic model refresh",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create and manage workspaces and assets — Identify when a gateway is required",
      "prompt": "A shared enterprise semantic model imports data from a SQL Server that is reachable only inside the company private network. Refresh succeeds in Power BI Desktop on the analyst’s VPN but fails after publishing to the service. The source has no public endpoint and central IT must govern connections. What should the team implement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Install an on-premises data gateway in standard mode on an always-on host with SQL Server network access, register the SQL source, and map the semantic model to that connection."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Change the Power BI report filter pane to show all rows, then schedule refresh in the service."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Install only Power BI Desktop on the report author’s laptop and leave it disconnected when refresh runs."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change the SQL Server Import model to a DAX calculated table and remove the data source credentials."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V61-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V61-003",
      "topic": "Personalized report visuals without edit rights",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Enable personalization in a report",
      "prompt": "A team publishes a governed report to read-only business consumers. Each consumer should be able to change a supported visual’s dimension or visual type for their own exploration, but they must not edit the shared report definition. They also want to reopen the personalized arrangement later. What is the most appropriate design?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Grant every consumer Contributor access to the workspace and instruct them to republish the report under the same name."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Turn on visual personalization for the report and have viewers save their modifications as personal bookmarks."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use drillthrough buttons and expect them to automatically save changes to the visual type."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Require each viewer to export the report as PDF, edit the PDF visualization, and re-upload it as a Power BI report."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V61-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V61-004",
      "topic": "Accessible report tab order and data-rich tooltips",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Design and configure Power BI reports for accessibility",
      "prompt": "A Power BI report contains a decorative logo, three KPI visuals, and a trend chart. Screen-reader users report that keyboard focus visits the decorative logo first and a critical exception explanation is available only in a hover tooltip. Which TWO author actions best address the accessibility defects without removing the KPI visuals?",
      "type": "multi",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Reorder keyboard tab navigation to follow the analytical reading sequence, and remove purely decorative objects from tab order."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Place critical exception meaning in persistently visible report content or an accessible textual alternative, not solely a hover tooltip."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Switch all KPI backgrounds to identical colors so users cannot distinguish sections by visual contrast."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Force a tooltip to appear automatically for every screen-reader user by enabling a visual-level sort."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-001",
      "topic": "Layout bookmarks without overwriting user filter selections",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Configure bookmarks",
      "prompt": "A Power BI report has a Region slicer, a matrix, and two detail charts. The business wants a button to toggle the two charts between shown and hidden. A user selects Region=West and must retain West after toggling, even though the bookmarks were recorded while Region=East. The visual arrangement must change consistently. Which bookmark configuration is most appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep Data enabled in both layout bookmarks and capture all report visuals."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Disable Data in the layout bookmarks, capture the desired Display states, and scope visuals carefully."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a report page tooltip for the two detail charts and remove the bookmarks."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Enable Data but disable Display in both bookmarks."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-002",
      "topic": "Hidden but synced report slicer",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Configure sync slicers",
      "prompt": "A three-page regional performance report has a Region slicer on Overview. Managers want Region selection to filter Metrics and Details too, but the slicer must occupy no space on Details. They must still be able to change Region from Overview and have every page follow it. What should you configure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Sync the Region slicer to Metrics and Details; clear Visible only for Details."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep a report page-level visual interaction only on Overview."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Hide Region in the semantic model and remove it from the slicer."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a drillthrough page for Details with no drillthrough fields."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-003",
      "topic": "Mobile layout versus standard web page",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Design reports for mobile devices",
      "prompt": "An author designs a Power BI phone layout, publishes the report, and verifies it in the Power BI Android app. A reviewer then opens the same report in a mobile phone web browser and sees the standard canvas instead of the phone arrangement. Which interpretation best fits documented behavior?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The report was not published because phone layouts require separate publication."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The Android app must have ignored the saved mobile layout due to a fixed pixel size."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "The phone web browser generally uses the standard report view; mobile-optimized layouts display in Power BI mobile apps."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Phone layouts are available only for dashboards, not Power BI reports."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-004",
      "topic": "Operational pixel-perfect multipage invoice distribution",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create reports — Choose when to use a paginated report",
      "prompt": "An insurer must issue 42,000 customer invoices each month. Each invoice needs a precise print layout with a repeating table header across multiple pages, page numbers, and PDF export. Recipients will not interact with slicers. Which report approach most directly meets these requirements?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a regular interactive canvas page with drill-down visuals and rely on each consumer to print it."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Publish a dashboard with pinned invoices and enable automatic page refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Author a paginated report with Report Builder using invoice grouping, page headers and controlled export."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a Power BI personal bookmark per invoice and email its browser URL."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-005",
      "topic": "RLS union across two roles",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure and govern Power BI items — Configure row-level security group membership",
      "prompt": "A report has two validated RLS roles on DimRegion: NorthOnly allows North, and SouthOnly allows South. A Viewer is mistakenly assigned to both roles. The report author assumes the rules intersect so the Viewer should see no rows. What is the actual consequence?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The roles are additive, so the Viewer can see North and South data."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Power BI rejects the second role assignment and always applies the first role."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "The roles intersect, leaving only rows labeled both North and South."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "The Viewer becomes a workspace Member and RLS no longer applies."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-006",
      "topic": "Semantic model Build rights versus row-level data access",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure and govern Power BI items — Configure access to semantic models",
      "prompt": "An analytics team wants users to create their own reports from a certified Sales semantic model, but each user must see only customers in their assigned territory. The users have read access and will be granted Build; a role-level territory filter already exists but has not been assigned in the service. Which action is essential before granting self-service access?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Set every sensitive customer column to Hidden and omit the security role membership."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Assign users to the appropriate territory RLS role, test access as a consuming user, and then grant needed Build permission."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Give everyone workspace Contributor rights because Contributor always enforces RLS."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Only apply a report-level slicer by Territory and give users Build."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-007",
      "topic": "Visual calculation scoped to report visual",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create reports — Create visual calculations by using DAX",
      "prompt": "A finance report shows FiscalYear rows in a matrix with Sales Amount and Total Product Cost as existing measures. The author wants a Profit column, calculated from the visual’s displayed values, for this matrix only. Other reports should not inherit this calculation. Which solution best matches that scope?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add Profit as a calculated column to the Sales fact table."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Add a reusable Profit measure to the published semantic model."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create a visual calculation Profit = [Sales Amount] - [Total Product Cost] on the matrix."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Add a custom page tooltip that subtracts two metric labels."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-008",
      "topic": "App audience navigation versus security boundaries",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create and manage workspaces and assets — Configure or update an app",
      "prompt": "A Power BI workspace app has Finance and Operations audiences. An author hides a Finance-only report from Operations navigation but enables Allow access to hidden content. An Operations user already has a direct link and sufficient item permissions. The author assumes hidden navigation alone blocks the report. What is the correct governance response?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep the hidden-content setting because navigation hiding automatically revokes all underlying item permissions."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace app audiences with a bookmarks menu to lock down finance data."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Turn on personalized visuals so the Operations user can remove the Finance report."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Disable access to hidden content where appropriate and audit direct item access and underlying model security."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-009",
      "topic": "Sensitivity label is not row-level filter",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure and govern Power BI items — Apply sensitivity labels",
      "prompt": "A company labels its Sales semantic model Confidential and publishes a single app to regional teams. Users can open the report and currently see every region. The report owner expects the Confidential label to show only the user’s region. Which correction is necessary?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Implement and test regional RLS, retaining the appropriate sensitivity label for classification and supported export protection."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Choose a darker theme for the app because labels use theme colors for regional filtering."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Require users to export to PDF; the sensitivity label filters each exported row to their region."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Promote the semantic model instead of configuring roles; promotion automatically restricts its data."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V62-010",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V62-010",
      "topic": "Discriminate DAX versus rendering slowness with Performance Analyzer",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Optimize model performance — Identify poorly performing measures, relationships, and visuals",
      "prompt": "A page refresh takes eight seconds after a Region slicer change. The team suspects an expensive DAX measure, but they have no timings. They need to determine whether the delay is dominated by query execution or the visual draw. What should they capture first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Disable all model relationships and compare a single unfiltered screenshot."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Switch the report to DirectQuery without testing and assume its latency is lower."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Record the slicer interaction with Performance Analyzer and compare each visual’s DAX query versus Visual display durations."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Hide slow visuals in the Selection pane and conclude that every DAX query is efficient."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V64-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V64-001",
      "topic": "RLS with Viewer and Build permission; Analyze in Excel",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure and govern Power BI items — Implement row-level security role membership",
      "prompt": "A regional sales semantic model uses a tested RLS role that filters Sales to each regional Entra group. Analysts must explore the governed semantic model through Analyze in Excel but must never see sales for other regions. They currently access the workspace as Contributors. Which TWO changes provide the intended least-privilege design? (Select TWO.)",
      "type": "multi",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Replace the analysts’ Contributor workspace assignment with Viewer or app-based read-only access."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Assign the Entra groups to the published model’s RLS roles, and grant Build permission for Analyze in Excel as needed."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Keep Contributors and verify the role’s DAX expression; correct DAX automatically causes RLS to apply to Contributors."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Apply a sensitivity label to the report instead of assigning RLS role members."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V64-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V64-002",
      "topic": "Synced slicer applies to an unseen report page",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Configure sync slicers",
      "prompt": "An executive report has Overview, Operations, and Audit pages. A fiscal-quarter slicer must be visible on Overview and Operations but hidden on Audit; all three pages must nevertheless use the most recently selected quarter. Audit has its own category slicer that must remain independent. What should the author configure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Copy the quarter slicer only onto Overview and Operations, then save the selected quarter as a report bookmark for Audit."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Synchronize the quarter slicer across all three pages, turn off its Visible setting on Audit, and leave the category slicer unsynced."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set the Audit quarter slicer to Visible but turn off its Sync setting; visibility alone propagates selections."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Turn off all edit interactions on Overview and Operations; this carries quarter selections across report pages."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V64-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V64-003",
      "topic": "Sensitivity label export protection versus Power BI service permissions",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure and govern Power BI items — Apply sensitivity labels and configure item-level access",
      "prompt": "A financial team wants authorized viewers to open a governed report in the Power BI service, while supported exported Excel and PDF copies should inherit a Confidential label with file-protection policies. A security engineer proposes applying a sensitivity label as the *sole* way to restrict access in the service. Which TWO statements are accurate? (Select TWO.)",
      "type": "multi",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Apply an appropriate configured sensitivity label to the supported Power BI artifact so protection can accompany supported exports."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep Power BI item/workspace permissions and any model RLS separately configured because a sensitivity label does not itself revoke service access."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Once Confidential is applied, the label alone denies the report in the service to every person lacking rights to decrypt an exported Excel file."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Guarantee that every Power BI CSV export carries the same encrypted sensitivity label as supported Excel export paths."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V64-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V64-004",
      "topic": "Drillthrough destination with correct entity filter field",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Enhance reports for usability and storytelling — Configure drillthrough navigation",
      "prompt": "On a sales summary page, selecting Store 041 should navigate to a dedicated Store Detail page and show only its transactions while preserving the active Year slicer when requested. A normal page-navigation button opens Store Detail, but it shows every store. Which redesign best meets the requirement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add Store[StoreID] to the detail page’s Drillthrough filters, use the matching store field on the source visual, and configure a Drillthrough action with the applicable filter behavior."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set the detail page as a custom report tooltip without adding any drillthrough filter fields."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a regular page navigation button and assume the page’s Year slicer automatically constrains Store to the clicked bar."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace StoreID with Sales Amount in the drillthrough field well; matching totals will infer the correct store."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V66-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V66-001",
      "topic": "Folder combine — archive filtering and file-lineage preservation",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transform and load the data",
      "prompt": "A Power BI Import model ingests daily CSV orders from `\\\\fileserver\\Orders\\`. The Folder connector also returns archived subfolder files and a temporary `.csv` containing yesterday's partial extract. The auditor needs only the approved `Current\\` files and must trace each loaded row to its source filename. Which procedure is safest?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Select Combine immediately; Power Query excludes subfolders and temporary files by default."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Filter the folder file listing to the approved path and naming pattern before invoking Combine; retain source filename from the combined output."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Combine every file, then hide the Filename field in the report visual to remove audit exceptions."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Only filter the final sales fact by OrderDate after Combine because the date uniquely identifies the file."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V66-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V66-002",
      "topic": "Schema drift — missing optional column in CSV batch",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transform and load the data",
      "prompt": "An M ingestion function processes three approved CSV exports with `OrderID` and `Amount`; only the latest file contains optional `PromoCode`. The final standardized table must always expose all three fields in that order and set absent PromoCode to null, not drop the column or invent codes. Which M step best normalizes each parsed file table `t` before appending?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "code": "Table.SelectColumns(t, {\"OrderID\",\"Amount\",\"PromoCode\"}, MissingField.UseNull)",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "`Table.SelectColumns(t, {\"OrderID\",\"Amount\",\"PromoCode\"}, MissingField.UseNull)`"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "`Table.SelectColumns(t, {\"OrderID\",\"Amount\",\"PromoCode\"}, MissingField.Ignore)`"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "`Table.RemoveColumns(t, {\"PromoCode\"}, MissingField.Ignore)`"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "`Table.SelectColumns(t, {\"OrderID\",\"Amount\",\"PromoCode\"})`"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V69-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V69-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-006",
      "topic": "RLS: workspace edit roles vs viewer enforcement",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "prompt": "A Power BI semantic model has a role that filters Sales to the current user’s assigned territory. Dana is assigned this RLS role but is also a Contributor in the workspace containing the semantic model. Testing as the role filters correctly, yet Dana can see all territories in ordinary report use. Which change is required to enforce this model RLS for Dana without granting editing rights?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Change Dana to the workspace Viewer role and retain appropriate role membership."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Add another RLS role with the same territory predicate while Dana remains Contributor."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change the Sales territory key to text while preserving Contributor access."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Give Dana workspace Admin permission and apply the role twice."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V69-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V69-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-002",
      "topic": "Many-to-many dimension relationships with validated bridge",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "prompt": "A company assigns each salesperson to multiple sales territories, and each territory can have multiple salespeople. FactSales records TerritoryKey per transaction; management needs Revenue by Salesperson, including all assigned territories. The model includes unique DimSalesperson and DimTerritory keys. There is no business rule for attributing overlapping territory revenue to individual salespeople. Which model action is defensible without falsely claiming nonoverlapping salesperson totals?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Create a distinct SalespersonTerritory bridge and configure validated filter propagation to territory sales, while explicitly warning that totals across salespeople may overlap."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Relate salesperson names directly to FactSales[TerritoryKey] with 1:* cardinality and claim totals are additive across salespeople."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Duplicate each transaction into FactSales once per salesperson and sum all Salesperson totals as company revenue."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Keep a disconnected Salesperson slicer and assume it automatically filters revenue without a relationship or calculation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V71-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V71-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-010",
      "topic": "Incremental refresh partition boundaries and change detection",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transform and load the data",
      "prompt": "A SQL fact table has 90 million rows with OrderDate (datetime) and LastModified (datetime). The Power BI service must maintain incremental refresh partitions without duplicating orders exactly at partition boundaries. The source supports folding, and LastModified changes whenever an existing order is updated. Which design is appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Filter OrderDate >= RangeStart and OrderDate <= RangeEnd; detect changes using OrderDate."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Filter OrderDate >= RangeStart and OrderDate < RangeEnd using Date/Time parameters; separately use LastModified for Detect data changes."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Filter OrderDate > RangeStart and OrderDate < RangeEnd; detect changes using LastModified."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Download the entire SQL table, then apply a nonfolding text filter on OrderDate; use LastModified as the partition key."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V71-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V71-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-011",
      "topic": "Field parameters for explicit measures and drillthrough limitations",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create reports",
      "prompt": "A report must let viewers switch a matrix between the explicit measures [Net Revenue] and [Gross Margin %] using a slicer. The matrix also needs a product-level drillthrough page filtered by ProductKey. A developer tries to place a field parameter in the drillthrough field well. What should you change?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep the field parameter for choosing explicit measures in the matrix Values well; put the actual ProductKey field in the drillthrough field well."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace the two explicit measures with implicit Sum aggregations, then use the parameter itself for drillthrough."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove field parameters and use a What-if numeric parameter with one page per measure; drillthrough by the parameter value."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use the parameter in the drillthrough field well and set the matrix cross-filter direction to Both."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-012",
      "topic": "Appending monthly exports with schema drift",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transform the data",
      "prompt": "A distributor receives one CSV export for each month. January has columns OrderID, NetAmount, Branch; February has OrderID, NetAmount, CampaignCode. Management requires a single table preserving all source rows and a null wherever an optional column does not exist. In Power Query M, what approach best meets the requirement without dropping attributes?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Select only OrderID and NetAmount before combining, then merge to a lookup on Branch."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Append the tables using Table.Combine without an enforced narrower columns list; inspect inferred union and type conversions."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use Table.NestedJoin with OrderID and expand both monthly tables, keeping only matching OrderID."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use a DAX UNION calculated table after import and assume different column orders and names automatically align by name."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-017",
      "topic": "Combining folder exports without contaminating the fact table",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transform and load the data",
      "prompt": "A folder stores 80 daily sales CSVs and also contains Archive subfolders, files beginning ~$, and a totals.csv file that already summarizes the daily exports. A sample file has the correct schema. The current Combine Files action doubles some revenue and sometimes fails on temporary files. Which first intervention gives a reproducible fix?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Change every sales column to Decimal Number after expanding; keep all folder entries."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Merge all files on their file names so totals.csv no longer repeats daily figures."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Filter the folder file inventory by path, name, extension, and intended input subset before invoking the sample-file transformation; then validate totals and schema."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Load all folder content as separate tables and enable Both on their semantic model relationships."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-003",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V22-ROLEPLAY-004",
      "topic": "Role-playing dates with simultaneous RLS-aware slicing",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Configure relationships",
      "prompt": "A Power BI sales model has one row per order and two foreign keys, OrderDateKey and ShipDateKey. Users must slice simultaneously by order month and ship month; regional RLS must remain enforced. The model currently uses a shared Date table with an active OrderDate and inactive ShipDate relationship. Which refactor best supports the requested independent filters?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use USERELATIONSHIP in every ShipDate measure and keep only one Date slicer for simultaneous independent date roles."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Enable Both on the existing OrderDate relationship and remove ShipDateKey from Sales."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create a many-to-many Date-to-Sales relationship on month name to make both roles active."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create role-specific Order Date and Ship Date dimension tables with validated active one-to-many paths to the two fact keys, then test RLS for relevant identities."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-004",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-002",
      "topic": "Prevent budget duplication across transaction-grain sales",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Design and implement a data model",
      "prompt": "FactSales has one row per invoice line (DepartmentKey, DateKey, Revenue). FactBudget has one row per DepartmentKey and FiscalMonth with BudgetAmount. The report must show actual and budget by fiscal month and department without multiplying monthly targets across sales lines. Which approach is best?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep separate facts at their native grains and relate them through compatible Department and Calendar/period dimensions; write grain-aware budget measures."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Join BudgetAmount onto every matching sales transaction, then SUM both Revenue and BudgetAmount."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Assign the full month budget to each day with sales and use MAX across all departments."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Append sales lines and monthly target rows into one table with no transaction-type distinction."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-005",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-001",
      "topic": "Row-level discount and quantity computation with SUMX",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create model calculations",
      "prompt": "FactLines has Quantity, UnitPrice, and DiscountRate (0 to 1) columns, with rows (2,50,0.10) and (1,100,0.20). A report must show net sales after each line discount, correctly responding to Product and Date filters. Which DAX measure matches the required 170 units of currency?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "SUM(FactLines[Quantity]) * SUM(FactLines[UnitPrice]) * (1 - AVERAGE(FactLines[DiscountRate]))"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "SUM(FactLines[UnitPrice]) - SUM(FactLines[DiscountRate])"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "SUMX(FactLines, FactLines[Quantity] * FactLines[UnitPrice] * (1 - FactLines[DiscountRate]))"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "COUNTROWS(FactLines) * MAX(FactLines[UnitPrice]) * (1 - MAX(FactLines[DiscountRate]))"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-006",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-003",
      "topic": "KEEPFILTERS versus filter replacement in CALCULATE",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create model calculations",
      "prompt": "A sales report has a Store[Region] slicer restricted to East and a valid Store(1) → Sales(*) relationship. A measure is `CALCULATE([Revenue], KEEPFILTERS(Store[Region] = \"West\"))`. Assume no store belongs to both regions and [Revenue] = SUM(Sales[Amount]). What does the measure return for the East-only selection?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "West revenue, because CALCULATE always overwrites a slicer even when KEEPFILTERS is applied."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "BLANK because East intersects West at no stores, leaving no rows to SUM."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "East revenue because KEEPFILTERS ignores the West filter argument when a slicer is present."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "All-regions revenue because KEEPFILTERS removes all existing Store filters from the evaluation context."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-007",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-008",
      "topic": "Workspace access bypass of otherwise valid RLS",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Apply row-level security",
      "prompt": "A sales model has an RLS role restricting Finance viewers to their own legal entity. A user is added to that RLS role but still sees all entities after being promoted to Contributor on the same workspace. The user needs to consume the report, not edit the dataset. Which change addresses the security exposure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Leave Contributor access and add a stricter second RLS role; RLS roles are automatically intersected for Contributors."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Hide LegalEntityKey from report view; hidden fields prevent disallowed records being queried."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set all model relationships to Both and keep Contributor access to force RLS on all users."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove the user’s elevated edit role, provide Viewer/appropriate app consumption permission, and validate the RLS role with Test as role."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-008",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-010",
      "topic": "Dynamic RLS over region entitlements with multiple memberships",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Apply row-level security",
      "prompt": "An organization stores UserRegion(UPN, RegionKey), with a user allowed two regions, and Sales has RegionKey. Report consumers access the dataset as Viewers. Security must filter by each viewer’s authenticated identity while allowing multiple region assignments and preventing row leakage. Which implementation should be designed and validated?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Define dynamic RLS on UserRegion using a UPN match to USERPRINCIPALNAME(), then validate active entitled-region filter propagation to Region/Sales with real test identities."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Create an unconnected username slicer that users can change and rely on it as the sole access control."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Allow users to join as workspace Members and expect RLS to apply because UPN matches the lookup."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use MIN(UserRegion[RegionKey]) so every user is permanently assigned to exactly one region, regardless of business entitlements."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-009",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-004",
      "topic": "Screen-reader accessible report with tooltip-only critical insight",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create reports",
      "prompt": "A Power BI report uses a red-only marker to indicate an SLA breach, places a crucial explanation solely in a hover tooltip, and makes keyboard users tab through 12 decorative shapes before the first KPI. What change most directly meets accessibility design guidance?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep the design but increase chart animation speed so nonvisual users can infer the red marker promptly."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Rely on the visual title alone, since tooltips are always read aloud by screen readers as users tab through marks."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Add a textual/iconic breach label and meaningful alt text, surface critical explanation outside the tooltip, and remove decorative elements from tab order."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Export a screenshot of the report page instead of publishing the interactive report."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V76-010",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V76-010",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-005",
      "topic": "Performance Analyzer isolates slow visual rendering versus DAX",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Optimize report performance",
      "prompt": "Users report that one geographic visual takes 3.5 seconds to appear after adjusting a slicer. Power BI Performance Analyzer records DAX query 110 ms, visual display 2,900 ms, Other 490 ms. Another simple table renders in 150 ms. Which diagnostic action is best justified first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "ADVANCED_APPLIED_SCENARIO",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Rewrite every measure using SUMX because almost all of the delay must be the semantic-model query."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Investigate the geographic visual’s rendering complexity and data point/shape burden, then remeasure after an isolated change."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set all semantic-model relationships to Both because this will reduce visual display time."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Disable refresh for every fact table, since longer visual display time proves refresh is the bottleneck."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-001",
      "topic": "PBIP Git collaboration and cache exclusions",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Manage workspace and semantic model development lifecycle",
      "prompt": "Two developers collaborate on a Power BI Desktop project stored in Git. One changes a measure and another changes a report visual. After a merge, a teammate proposes checking in every file, including .pbi/cache.abf and .pbi/localSettings.json, to make builds reproducible. The team needs reviewable source history without committing machine-specific caches. What is the best policy?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Commit the cache.abf and localSettings.json alongside the measure edits; the local cache is the authoritative semantic-model definition."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace the report and semantic-model folders with a single PBIX binary on every commit; Git can semantically merge PBIX reports."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Commit the report and semantic-model project definition files, retain the recommended .gitignore exclusions for localSettings.json and cache.abf, and code-review the diff."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Commit only the .pbip shortcut file and discard the report and semantic-model definition folders, because Power BI regenerates all authored changes from the shortcut."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-011",
      "topic": "Additive RLS membership with two roles",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Implement row-level security",
      "prompt": "A Power BI Viewer is assigned to two semantic-model RLS roles through security groups. Role North filters Sales to Region = \"North\"; Role South filters Sales to Region = \"South\". The security team assumes that membership in both roles produces an intersection and therefore denies all rows. The source contains only North and South sales, and each role works individually. Which conclusion is correct?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The user sees no rows because Power BI always intersects RLS filters from different roles, and both regions cannot match at once."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The user can see North and South rows because different RLS role memberships are additive; redesign role assignment if this union violates entitlements."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "The user sees only North because Power BI prioritizes the role whose name sorts first when the user belongs to multiple roles."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Both roles are automatically disabled because RLS security groups cannot overlap on the same Viewer account."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-003",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-012",
      "topic": "Object-level security for a confidential field",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Configure object-level security",
      "prompt": "An HR Power BI semantic model stores EmployeeKey, Department, Salary and Headcount. Department managers may analyze employee counts and headcount by department, but must not query Salary or discover the Salary column metadata at all. The managers must retain access to permitted employees rather than having all rows removed. What security mechanism matches this exact requirement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Configure object-level security denying the Salary column to the managers’ read-only role, and separately validate any row restrictions that are needed."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set the Salary column to Hidden in the semantic model, because hidden columns cannot be queried by users with Build permissions."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Add an RLS expression such as Employee[Salary] > 0, which automatically conceals Salary values and its metadata while retaining all rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change the visual-level filters to exclude Salary from existing charts, because the underlying semantic model will then deny every Salary query."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-004",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-001",
      "topic": "DirectQuery cache versus source and responsiveness",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Choose connectivity mode",
      "prompt": "A call-center model must reflect new interactions without requiring a full Import model refresh. The fact table resides in a supported relational data source that can be queried on demand, and its data volume exceeds the available Import memory. Analysts also require interactive reports under a strict latency target. Which architecture choice and caveat are most defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use Import, because any new source row immediately appears in all imported model queries without a semantic-model refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use DirectQuery and assume subsecond report responses are guaranteed regardless of source indexing, network latency or visual query count."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a calculated table derived from Import data, which always sends live queries to the operational source when slicers change."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Evaluate DirectQuery for on-demand source access, then benchmark source performance, folding, model relationships and visual query patterns against latency requirements."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-005",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-001",
      "topic": "Dual dimension storage mode in composite model",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Choose storage mode",
      "prompt": "A composite Power BI model has a large DirectQuery FactSales table in SQL Server and a small conformed DimProduct table from the same SQL Server source. Users filter sales using DimProduct and also browse product-only slicers. Model designers want dimensions to be served efficiently alone while preserving regular relationships with DirectQuery facts where possible. Which storage-mode design should they evaluate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Make DimProduct an unrelated Import-only table; then assume it will automatically filter every DirectQuery FactSales row without a relationship."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set the shared-source DimProduct table to Dual where supported, validate its relationship with FactSales and measure cache/source behavior for both query patterns."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use Direct Lake on SQL as an arbitrary table storage mode in Power BI Desktop without a Fabric semantic model or supporting storage."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Force all tables to DirectQuery and remove DimProduct because dimensional slicers cannot be served efficiently in any composite model."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-006",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-002",
      "topic": "SELECTEDVALUE alternate behavior",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Write DAX measures",
      "prompt": "A report title measure is `Region Title = SELECTEDVALUE(Region[Name], \"Multiple or none\")`. A slicer first selects only East, then selects East and West, and finally clears the selection in a dimension containing East, West and Central. Assume no other filters restrict Region. What outputs should appear in the three states, respectively?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "East; East; East, because SELECTEDVALUE always returns the first sorted value when a selection has more than one distinct value."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "East; BLANK; BLANK, because the alternateResult parameter is ignored whenever the slicer is enabled."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "East; Multiple or none; Multiple or none, because exactly one distinct visible value is required to return Region[Name]."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "East; East,West; East,West,Central, because SELECTEDVALUE automatically concatenates every value into a CSV-like string."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-007",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-004",
      "topic": "CALCULATE context transition in calculated columns",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Evaluate row and filter context",
      "prompt": "Customer has two unique rows CustomerKey 1 and 2, with a valid active 1:* relationship to Sales[CustomerKey]. Sales contains (CustomerKey 1, Amount 10) and (CustomerKey 2, Amount 20). In an Import-mode calculated column on Customer, the author writes `CALCULATE(SUM(Sales[Amount]))`. Assume no other data or filters. What are the two calculated-column values?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "30 and 30, because CALCULATE does not transition row context to filter context within any calculated column."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "10 and 10, because the first Customer row becomes a filter for every subsequent Customer row during model processing."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "BLANK and BLANK, because a calculated column can never invoke CALCULATE with a fact-table aggregation."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "10 and 20, because CALCULATE performs context transition for the current Customer row and the relationship filters matching Sales rows."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-008",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-001",
      "topic": "Incremental refresh partition boundary correctness",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Configure incremental refresh",
      "prompt": "An Import fact table uses Power Query RangeStart and RangeEnd Date/Time parameters for incremental refresh. Source rows can have timestamps exactly equal to the boundary between two adjacent partitions. A developer uses `OrderDate >= RangeStart and OrderDate <= RangeEnd` for every partition and finds duplicated boundary rows. Which correction is justified?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use one inclusive and one exclusive boundary, for example OrderDate >= RangeStart and OrderDate < RangeEnd, then validate query folding and partition results."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use both bounds inclusive but overwrite the model with DISTINCTCOUNT(OrderID), which physically removes duplicated records from partitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove all date filtering from the Power Query fact query, because the service partitions and deduplicates rows regardless of the specified RangeStart/RangeEnd filter."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Convert RangeStart and RangeEnd to text and compare lexicographically, ensuring records never match two time windows even with inconsistent formats."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V77-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V77-009",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-006",
      "topic": "Field parameter measure requirements",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Create dynamic report visual analysis",
      "prompt": "A report reader must switch a chart’s value from Net Revenue to Margin Percentage through a field-parameter slicer. Net Revenue currently exists only as the implicit aggregation of Sales[NetAmount], while Margin Percentage is an explicit DAX measure. The author tries to add both to a field parameter, but the implicit aggregation is unsupported. What is the most suitable repair?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_MULTI_CONSTRAINT_EXAM_STYLE__PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add Sales[NetAmount] as an implicit Sum measure to the parameter anyway; field parameters automatically support implicit aggregations."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Define an explicit Net Revenue DAX measure and include that measure alongside Margin Percentage in the field parameter, then test formatting and total behavior."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Build a disconnected table of measure names and expect its slicer to rewrite all visual values without any explicit DAX selection logic."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace the existing Margin Percentage measure with a calculated column and use a field parameter of text captions to force automatic numeric aggregation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-018",
      "topic": "Folder ingestion: schema drift",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Folder ingestion: schema drift",
      "prompt": "A monthly folder contains three CSV exports with CustomerID and Amount. Only the newest file has DiscountCode; one export uses an extra AuditNote column. Finance must retain all rows and every optional field in the combined result, with nulls where a file lacks a field. All files use the same delimiter and encoding. Which transformation is defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep the generated Transform Sample File query selecting CustomerID and Amount, then append the output."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Read files into structured tables and use Table.Combine without a restricted columns list; validate resulting types and nullable columns."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Inner join all files on CustomerID to deduplicate customers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use Table.Distinct on Amount after combining the files."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-019",
      "topic": "Unpivot future month columns",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Unpivot future month columns",
      "prompt": "A spreadsheet extract has fixed identifying fields ProductID and Territory plus monthly amount columns Jan, Feb, and Mar. New month columns will be added every period. The query must produce [ProductID,Territory,Month,Amount] and automatically include months not yet present. Which operation should the author choose?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Unpivot only Jan, Feb and Mar; future columns will be detected from their names."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Pivot ProductID while aggregating Amount to text."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Select ProductID and Territory, then choose Unpivot Other Columns; validate new months and null handling."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Group by ProductID and Territory before unpivoting, keeping one arbitrary monthly amount."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-003",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-020",
      "topic": "Merge row multiplication",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Merge row multiplication",
      "prompt": "Orders has 1,000 unique OrderID rows. A lookup advertised as one row per CustomerID actually contains two active addresses for 40 customers. Expanding the address after a left merge makes the Orders query larger. The report needs exactly one *current* address for each order, based on EffectiveDate. What should be fixed?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Deduplicate Orders by CustomerID before merging, even though multiple orders per customer are valid."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Change the merge to Full Outer and apply Remove Duplicates to the resulting OrderID field."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use Append instead of Merge because Append always preserves exactly the number of Orders rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Choose the latest eligible address per CustomerID at the required business date, verify key uniqueness, and then merge at order grain."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-004",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-021",
      "topic": "Parsing locale-specific decimal",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Parsing locale-specific decimal",
      "prompt": "A German subsidiary provides a text column NetPrice with values like 1.234,50, while the organization's gateway machine uses an English locale. A naïve automatic type-change parses different values or raises errors. What is the most robust transformation before loading a numeric measure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Change NetPrice to Decimal Number using Change Type with Locale and the known German source locale; inspect parse errors."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Remove every comma and period, then convert to Whole Number."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Leave NetPrice as text and let SUM implicitly convert it per chart."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Set the Windows display language on every report consumer's computer."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-005",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-022",
      "topic": "Query folding and early filters",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Query folding and early filters",
      "prompt": "A SQL Server order-lines table has 90 million records. Power Query currently invokes a custom row-by-row function before filtering to the most recent quarter and selecting four columns. Refresh time is excessive. Which experiment best tests whether work can be pushed to SQL Server?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add Table.Buffer immediately after the source step and assume all later steps now fold."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Move selective supported date filtering and column projection before the nonfoldable custom function; inspect folding indicators and the actual source query."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Convert the source to a CSV export to ensure the SQL Server engine evaluates M expressions."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Duplicate the entire query ten times so each refresh evaluates fewer rows automatically."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-006",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-025",
      "topic": "Privacy Firewall cross-source joins",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Privacy Firewall cross-source joins",
      "prompt": "An analyst combines a confidential payroll table from an internal SQL source with a publicly hosted currency-rate service. A colleague advises disabling all privacy levels globally to make the join run faster. Security requirements prohibit leaking raw payroll rows to the public endpoint. Which decision should be taken?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Ignore the firewall because Power Query never transfers any source data between connectors."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Give both sources Public privacy classification to eliminate warnings without assessing sensitivity."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Keep correct privacy classifications, examine allowable mashup strategies, and redesign or stage conversion locally if the firewall blocks an unsafe cross-source plan."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Set every payroll field to Hidden in the semantic model before refresh."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-007",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-026",
      "topic": "Power Query optional missing field",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Power Query optional missing field",
      "prompt": "Two quarterly extracts have different schemas: one has [InvoiceID,CostCenter], the other only [InvoiceID]. Compliance requires a CostCenter column in both outputs with null for unavailable data, not a missing-column refresh error. Which M transformation provides the documented behavior?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Table.SelectColumns(Source,{\"InvoiceID\",\"CostCenter\"},MissingField.Error)."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Table.RemoveColumns(Source,{\"CostCenter\"},MissingField.Ignore)."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Table.RenameColumns(Source,{{\"InvoiceID\",\"CostCenter\"}})."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Table.SelectColumns(Source,{\"InvoiceID\",\"CostCenter\"},MissingField.UseNull)."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-008",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-027",
      "topic": "Dates with embedded time",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Dates with embedded time",
      "prompt": "A fact table contains SQL datetime EventAt values such as 2026-07-01 23:45 and 2026-07-02 00:05. A Date dimension has midnight-only dates. A direct relationship on EventAt unexpectedly puts many events under Blank. What is the most reliable modeling preparation?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Create a date-only key from EventAt in Power Query or source, ensuring compatible types, and relate that to the unique date key."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set Date dimension's display format to hide hours, leaving fact timestamps unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Enable bi-directional filtering between Date and Fact and assume this coerces timestamps to dates."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use both relationships at once: EventAt to Date plus a second EventAt to Time."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-009",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-029",
      "topic": "Data profiling sample trap",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Data profiling sample trap",
      "prompt": "A Power Query table has 800,000 records. Column quality displays no nulls, but an audit finds nulls near the end of the source. The query editor had been profiling the first 1,000 rows. What should the analyst do before certifying data completeness?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Conclude the source is clean because Column quality automatically scans every record."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Change profile calculations to evaluate the entire dataset, assess refresh impact, and supplement with source-level null-count checks."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Delete the first 1,000 rows to force the profile to use only later records."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Rename the column before profiling to invalidate all cached statistics."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-010",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-010",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-035",
      "topic": "Type conversions after union",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Type conversions after union",
      "prompt": "Five branch extracts append successfully, but one branch provides Quantity as the text string \"N/A\" and all others provide integers. A downstream integer conversion raises errors, and operations want to distinguish missing measurement from zero units sold. Which handling is safest?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Replace every conversion error with numeric zero to keep SUM reports simple."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Drop the branch with the incompatible rows and leave no audit record."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Standardize N/A to null using an explicit source rule, set numeric type, log invalid raw values, and distinguish null from zero in downstream measures."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change Quantity to a categorical code and SUM over the string with implicit casts."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-011",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-011",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V08-FILES-038",
      "topic": "Incremental-refresh source folding",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Incremental-refresh source folding",
      "prompt": "An imported Orders query has RangeStart and RangeEnd parameters, but a nonfoldable transformation executes before the date filter. Preview looks fine, yet the initial service refresh repeatedly scans the full source for partitions. Which change should be prioritized?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Increase the policy's retention period because longer retention automatically folds DateTime filters."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Turn RangeStart and RangeEnd into text literals embedded in a DAX measure."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Define a refresh schedule at midnight; no source-date filter is needed for partitioned imports."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Apply an appropriate RangeStart-inclusive/RangeEnd-exclusive filter in the foldable part of the query and verify generated source predicates."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-012",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-012",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-003",
      "topic": "Budget and sales at incompatible grains",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Budget and sales at incompatible grains",
      "prompt": "A retailer has FactSales at individual ProductID–Date grain, and FactBudget at CategoryID–Month grain. Analysts want both Actual and Budget by category/month, but currently join FactBudget directly to FactSales by a category text field, multiplying targets across products. Which model architecture is sound?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Build conformed Category and Month dimensions, relate each fact at its own declared grain, and compare measures at a shared reporting grain."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Duplicate every budget row onto each product sale before summing the budget."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a many-to-many bidirectional relationship directly between both facts on CategoryID."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Calculate the budget as DISTINCTCOUNT of CategoryID in sales."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-013",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-013",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-006",
      "topic": "RLS-safe bridge between users and regions",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "RLS-safe bridge between users and regions",
      "prompt": "The security table contains UserUPN-to-RegionKey rows and some users have multiple regions. FactSales is keyed by RegionKey. A developer creates a bidirectional many-to-many filter between Users and FactSales and observes ambiguous security paths. Which redesign should be assessed?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Disable RLS and hide the Users table so only assigned regions are displayed."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Model a distinct Region dimension, a user-to-region entitlement bridge, and explicit tested security propagation to facts; validate under multiple identities."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Copy every user name into the Sales fact and expose it to report viewers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Assign each user to all available RLS roles; Power BI intersects those roles to minimize data access."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-014",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-014",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V22-ROLEPLAY-010",
      "topic": "Surrogate key for Type 2 dimension",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Surrogate key for Type 2 dimension",
      "prompt": "ProductCode P17 moved from Category Old to Category New on 1 June. Transactions before and after the change must report against their historical categories. A dimension currently stores two P17 rows, but the Sales fact links to ProductCode and the relationship can't be one-to-many. Which ETL modeling change is needed?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep ProductCode as the only relationship key and choose Many-to-Many to retain both versions."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Delete the old category record and rewrite all history as New."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create version-specific surrogate ProductKeys and resolve each transaction to the effective version during ETL; relate FactSales.ProductKey to Dimension.ProductKey."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace ProductCode with the month number in both tables."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-015",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-015",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-007",
      "topic": "Single-direction path and related slicers",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Single-direction path and related slicers",
      "prompt": "Sales and Returns are separate facts, each linked to Product and Date dimensions with standard 1:* single-direction relationships. A developer makes every relationship Both just so selecting Sales rows filters a Return chart; model queries become ambiguous. Which adjustment should be tried first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Join Returns to Sales on free-text product names and set many-to-many cardinality."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Make each dimension-to-fact relationship bidirectional to emulate a relational OLTP schema."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Hide Product and Date dimensions so report visuals can only filter by Sales columns."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use common dimension slicers for both facts; if a specific visual interaction is needed, configure the report interaction or a scoped DAX calculation rather than blindly making all paths Both."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-016",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-016",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-003",
      "topic": "Unknown member for missing dimension keys",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Unknown member for missing dimension keys",
      "prompt": "An Import model has DimStore containing StoreKey 1 and 2, while FactSales contains StoreKey 1, 2 and 99. A matrix grouped by StoreName shows a '(Blank)' row with sales. Management wants unknown store revenue retained and investigated, not suppressed. What is the correct interpretation and action?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "StoreKey 99 violates referential integrity; retain the revenue, repair the source/dimension mapping or implement a governed Unknown member and audit the discrepancy."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Power BI duplicates the StoreKey 99 row because both cross-filter paths are active."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Automatically remove every fact row whose StoreKey is absent in the dimension."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change StoreName formatting to nonblank; model joins use formatted display values."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-017",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-017",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-008",
      "topic": "Badly configured Assume referential integrity",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Badly configured Assume referential integrity",
      "prompt": "Two DirectQuery SQL tables use a shared data source. Product is intended as a lookup, but fact ProductKey contains occasional nulls and missing Product records. Someone proposes enabling Assume Referential Integrity to make joins faster. What should the modeler do?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Enable it: DirectQuery always uses a left join even with the property enabled."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Do not enable it until one-to-many key integrity and non-null fact keys are verified or repaired; benchmark the join only after prerequisites hold."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Enable it only on a many-to-many cross-source relationship; these are required prerequisites."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Switch all tables to Import, because no SQL DirectQuery source supports the property."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-018",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-018",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V22-ROLEPLAY-011",
      "topic": "Role-playing Date for order and shipment",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Role-playing Date for order and shipment",
      "prompt": "FactDelivery contains OrderDateKey and ShipDateKey. The report needs order revenue by OrderDate and shipment revenue by ShipDate in separate visuals, with both pages supporting their own time slicers. A single Date table has an active OrderDate relationship and inactive ShipDate relationship. Which strategy best avoids silently treating shipped revenue as ordered revenue?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Activate both relationships between the same two tables and assume Power BI uses each context automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a measure with SUM on ShipDateKey as if date keys were revenue values."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Define an explicit Ship Revenue measure using the appropriate existing inactive relationship with USERELATIONSHIP, or use separate role-playing date dimensions where independent slicers are needed."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Delete the OrderDate relationship and keep ShipDate only; order-date analysis will stay correct through auto-detection."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-019",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-019",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-004",
      "topic": "Fact grain before distinct order count",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Fact grain before distinct order count",
      "prompt": "A sales table stores one row per OrderID–LineNumber. Order 100 contains three lines, order 200 one line, and order 300 two lines. A KPI called Orders must display 3 while Revenue sums all six lines. Which measures avoid a grain error?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Orders = COUNTROWS(Sales); Revenue = DISTINCTCOUNT(Sales[Amount])."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Orders = SUM(Sales[OrderID]); Revenue = COUNTROWS(Sales)."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Orders = COUNT(Sales[OrderID]); Revenue = AVERAGE(Sales[Amount])."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Orders = DISTINCTCOUNT(Sales[OrderID]); Revenue = SUM(Sales[Amount]), with data-quality checks on null OrderID."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-020",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-020",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-011",
      "topic": "Limited cross-source relationship",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Limited cross-source relationship",
      "prompt": "A composite model joins Import DimCustomer to DirectQuery FactInvoices in a different source group, with 1:* cardinality. A developer uses RELATED(DimCustomer[Segment]) in a calculated expression over FactInvoices and sees restrictions. Why is the usual dimension lookup assumption unsafe here?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The cross-source-group relationship is limited and does not support regular table expansion or RELATED in that way; reassess model/source grouping or use supported measures."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Import DimCustomer is always automatically transferred into the DirectQuery source on refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "RELATED works only when both tables have no relationships at all."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "A 1:* label guarantees a regular relationship even across distinct source groups."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-021",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-021",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-006",
      "topic": "Two fact tables and a shared conformed dimension",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Two fact tables and a shared conformed dimension",
      "prompt": "FactInventorySnapshot stores one stock balance per ProductID at each midnight, while FactSales stores transactional line amounts. A manager wants a monthly stock trend and monthly sales on the same page. A developer proposes appending snapshot balances into Sales as if they were additional sales transactions. What is the correct modeling decision?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Append them: balances and transactional amounts share a ProductID and therefore have identical grain."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep separate facts related to shared Product and Date dimensions, calculate stock as a snapshot measure and sales as transaction sums."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Join each daily snapshot to all sales lines; SUM of the resulting Balance column produces month-end stock."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Discard Date in the inventory snapshot because stock balances do not change over time."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-022",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-022",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-005",
      "topic": "KEEPFILTERS intersections",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "KEEPFILTERS intersections",
      "prompt": "FactSales has three rows (Color,Amount): (Red,10), (Blue,20), (Green,30). A report visual is currently filtered to Red and Blue, and [Revenue] = SUM(FactSales[Amount]). What does `CALCULATE([Revenue], KEEPFILTERS(FactSales[Color] = \"Blue\"))` return, assuming no other filters?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "30, because KEEPFILTERS removes the existing Red/Blue selection before applying its predicate."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "20, because the existing Red/Blue set intersects with Blue to leave only the Blue row."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "60, because CALCULATE always ignores filter context for the entire table."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "BLANK, because KEEPFILTERS can't be used with a Boolean filter argument."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-023",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-023",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-006",
      "topic": "Denominator that preserves territory and removes product filters",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Denominator that preserves territory and removes product filters",
      "prompt": "A report shows sales by Product[Brand] within a selected Territory. [Revenue] is SUM(Sales[Amount]); Product filters reach Sales through a normal dimension relationship. An analyst wants each brand's percentage of total revenue for *all brands within the current Territory*, preserving other page filters. Which denominator is appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Revenue], REMOVEFILTERS(Territory))"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "SUM(Product[Brand]) in the model as the denominator of the percentage"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Revenue], REMOVEFILTERS(Product[Brand]))"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "CALCULATE([Revenue], ALL(Sales))"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-024",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-024",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-001",
      "topic": "Iterating rows vs averaging unit prices",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Iterating rows vs averaging unit prices",
      "prompt": "An order-line fact has (Quantity,UnitPrice) values (2,10) and (1,100). The order's recorded total should be 120. A developer writes `SUM(Fact[Quantity]) * AVERAGE(Fact[UnitPrice])` and gets a different result. Which measure evaluates extended line amounts correctly even when prices vary?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "SUMX(Fact, Fact[Quantity] * Fact[UnitPrice])"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "COUNTROWS(Fact) * MAX(Fact[UnitPrice])"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "SUM(Fact[Quantity]) * SUM(Fact[UnitPrice])"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "AVERAGEX(Fact, Fact[Quantity] * Fact[UnitPrice])"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-025",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-025",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V29-AGG-001",
      "topic": "DIVIDE blank result and visual suppression",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DIVIDE blank result and visual suppression",
      "prompt": "A KPI is `Margin % = DIVIDE([Margin],[Revenue])`. For a brand with [Margin]=0 and [Revenue]=0, the visual unexpectedly omits the brand instead of showing 0%. The business wants zero when the denominator is zero, subject to an explicit reporting policy. What change exactly accomplishes this?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Replace DIVIDE with `/` and assume division by zero always returns zero."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace DIVIDE with SUM([Margin]) and format as Percent."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Turn on bidirectional relationships so the blank appears as a zero."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use `DIVIDE([Margin],[Revenue],0)` and check whether including no-data categories changes presentation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-026",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-026",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V29-AGG-002",
      "topic": "Count blanks correctly",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Count blanks correctly",
      "prompt": "A Customer dimension contains CustomerKey values 1,2,3 and an inactive account flagged with CustomerKey BLANK due to staging defects. A compliance count must explicitly distinguish unique *nonblank* customer IDs from total distinct key values. Which interpretation is correct?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "COUNTROWS(Customer) always equals DISTINCTCOUNT(Customer[CustomerKey]) because keys are unique by definition."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "DISTINCTCOUNT(Customer[CustomerKey]) includes BLANK as a distinct value, whereas DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK excludes BLANK."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK counts each blank value twice as an error signal."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "COUNT(Customer[CustomerKey]) returns distinct nonblank values and ignores duplicate IDs."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-027",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-027",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-007",
      "topic": "Inactive relationship for delivered sales",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Inactive relationship for delivered sales",
      "prompt": "Calendar[Date] has an active relationship to Orders[OrderDate] and an inactive relationship to Orders[DeliveredDate]. A measure [Amount] sums Orders[TotalAmount]. On the same chart the analyst needs Delivered Amount by calendar month. Which expression makes the shipment date path explicit?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "`CALCULATE([Amount], USERELATIONSHIP(Orders[DeliveredDate],Calendar[Date]))`"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "`CALCULATE([Amount], KEEPFILTERS(Orders[OrderDate]))`"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "`RELATED(Calendar[Date])`"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "`COUNTROWS(Calendar)` in place of a sum of delivered sales revenue"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-028",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-028",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-008",
      "topic": "Disconnected scenario parameter and explicit selection",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Disconnected scenario parameter and explicit selection",
      "prompt": "Finance loads a disconnected table ScenarioRate with decimal values 0.05, 0.10, 0.15. The user chooses one rate in a slicer; [Base Revenue]=SUM(Sales[Amount]). What formula reliably uses the selected rate while applying a default of 0 if no single rate is selected?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Create a relationship between ScenarioRate[Rate] and Sales[Amount]; filter matching amount values."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "SUM(ScenarioRate[Rate]) * SUM(Sales[Amount])"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "[Base Revenue] * SELECTEDVALUE(ScenarioRate[Rate],0)"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "COUNTROWS(ScenarioRate) * [Base Revenue]"
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-029",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-029",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-009",
      "topic": "RLS and relationship activation caution",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "RLS and relationship activation caution",
      "prompt": "A model implements RLS on DimCustomer and has two customer relationship paths to a fact table. A measure author wants to swap the relationship used for security-relevant totals using `USERELATIONSHIP` inside CALCULATE, assuming all RLS filters will automatically follow whichever path the measure chooses. What review is essential?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Assume USERELATIONSHIP always changes the model's RLS security filter direction for every viewer."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Disable RLS during production to allow all date role measures to operate."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set every relationship to Both and assign all users to every role to prevent ambiguity."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Check documented USERELATIONSHIP restrictions with RLS and model security paths; redesign with explicit secure dimensions/measures and test as distinct users."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-030",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-030",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-010",
      "topic": "ALLSELECTED visual total interpretation",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "ALLSELECTED visual total interpretation",
      "prompt": "A visual displays Revenue by Brand and a slicer selects Brands A and B. The business wants the denominator to be the combined revenue of the brands included by that report selection, instead of each row's own Brand filter, while preserving slicer selections. Which technique should be evaluated?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "REMOVEFILTERS on every table in the model, because this always keeps slicer selections intact."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a visual-total measure with CALCULATE([Revenue],ALLSELECTED(Product[Brand])); validate other visual filters and layout-specific behavior."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use SUM(Product[Brand]) as denominator because the selected captions define the total."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use HASONEVALUE(Product[Brand]) as denominator because it returns aggregated revenue."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-031",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-031",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V29-AGG-003",
      "topic": "Calendar vs fiscal-year calculation",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Calendar vs fiscal-year calculation",
      "prompt": "A retail company defines its fiscal year to end on June 30. The [Net Sales] measure already respects a marked Date table. An author uses `TOTALYTD([Net Sales],Date[Date])` and assumes it rolls over on July 1 without additional configuration. What is the problem?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "TOTALYTD ignores date filters and therefore returns the full-company lifetime total."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The Date table must always be disconnected from Sales for TOTALYTD to work."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Without an explicit fiscal year-end convention or appropriate fiscal calendar handling, default YTD follows calendar year rather than June 30."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "TOTALYTD cannot be used with measures and accepts only calculated columns."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-032",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-032",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-012",
      "topic": "ISINSCOPE subtotal-specific logic",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "ISINSCOPE subtotal-specific logic",
      "prompt": "A matrix groups first by Region and then by Branch, showing a ratio measure. Branch rows need an annotation 'Branch', region subtotal rows need 'Region', and the grand total should read 'Overall'. A developer uses HASONEVALUE(Branch[Name]) and finds incorrect output when report filters leave one branch. Which technique is more robust?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use ISINSCOPE(Branch[Name]) first, then ISINSCOPE(Region[Name]), else Overall to distinguish grouping levels."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use DISTINCTCOUNT(Branch[Name])=1, which always identifies branch rows even in subtotals."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use SELECTEDVALUE(Branch[Name]) and assume a blank means the grand total."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Set both region and branch columns to Don't summarize so all subtotals become branch rows."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-033",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-033",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-002",
      "topic": "Context transition inside SUMX",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Context transition inside SUMX",
      "prompt": "A model has Customer related 1:* to Sales. In `SUMX(Customer, CALCULATE(SUM(Sales[Revenue])))`, analysts want the sum of each customer's revenue. A reviewer says CALCULATE is unnecessary because SUM alone always uses the current Customer row as a Sales filter. What is the accurate technical distinction?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "SUM inside an iterator automatically follows row context across relationships without any transition."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "CALCULATE removes the Customer row context, so every iteration returns the same grand total."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "SUMX cannot iterate dimension tables; it only supports fact tables with numeric fields."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "CALCULATE invokes context transition for the Customer row; the active relationship then filters matching Sales rows during each iteration."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-034",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-034",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-003",
      "topic": "Tooltip versus drillthrough tasks",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Tooltip versus drillthrough tasks",
      "prompt": "A service desk report displays incident counts by support team. Users need to inspect a filtered table, choose slicer values, and scroll through individual incidents after clicking a team. The designer suggests a rich report-page tooltip containing interactive controls. What is more suitable?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep a tooltip, because it supports direct slicer selection and keyboard tabbing in all cases."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a drillthrough detail page receiving the selected team context and place interactive visuals and slicers on that page."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use only alt text for the incident table; screen readers can query full interactive details from alt text."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Pin the tooltip page to a dashboard to turn its slicers interactive."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-035",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-035",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-005",
      "topic": "Prevent cross-highlighting on a KPI",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Prevent cross-highlighting on a KPI",
      "prompt": "On an executive report page, selecting a pie slice should filter a detail table but must not change the enterprise-wide target KPI card. Currently the KPI responds to the pie selection. Which report-authoring change meets this requirement without changing the underlying model relationships?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Disable all model relationships connecting the pie dimension to the fact table."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a DISTINCTCOUNT measure on the KPI because count measures ignore cross-selection automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change the pie chart's Edit Interactions behavior for the KPI to None, while keeping Filter for the detail table."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Hide the pie chart legend so users cannot select data points."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-036",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-036",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-008",
      "topic": "Bookmarks: state versus navigation",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Bookmarks: state versus navigation",
      "prompt": "A report has two bookmark buttons, 'Summary' and 'Details', used as tabs. After a user chooses a Year slicer, clicking Details resets the year to a saved value, even though switching views should preserve slicer choices. What bookmark design should be reviewed?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use bookmarks only to change display/visibility of relevant visuals and deselect Data in the bookmark settings where slicer state must persist."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Convert every slicer to a calculated column because slicers cannot coexist with bookmarks."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Make the bookmark names identical; Power BI ignores data restoration if names match."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Delete every page filter and activate bidirectional relationships globally."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-037",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-037",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-009",
      "topic": "Visual accessibility tab order",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Visual accessibility tab order",
      "prompt": "A procurement dashboard includes a meaningful chart, three slicers, a text explanation, and decorative logo shapes. A keyboard-only reviewer tabs through dozens of decorative shapes before reaching the primary controls. Which authoring fix targets this failure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add data labels to all bars but retain the existing tab navigation order."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set every decorative object's alt text to its file name and keep it in tab order."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Turn off chart accessibility so screen readers skip all visuals."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Arrange a logical keyboard tab order and remove purely decorative objects from tab navigation; add useful alt text to meaningful visuals."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-038",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-038",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-013",
      "topic": "Dynamic title and filter context",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Dynamic title and filter context",
      "prompt": "A matrix page can display a single department or multiple departments. Users want the title to say 'Sales — Finance' for exactly one selected department and 'Sales — all selected departments' otherwise. Which modeling/report approach handles both without relying on hard-coded bookmark captions?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use FIRSTNONBLANK(Department[Name],0) unconditionally; it returns a display label for multiple selections."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Create a DAX title measure using SELECTEDVALUE(Department[Name], \"all selected departments\") and bind the visual title by field value."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Rename the Department dimension based on the first report refresh; titles follow slicer choices automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use COUNTROWS(Sales) as the title field; numeric counts resolve to selected department names."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-039",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-039",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-014",
      "topic": "Conditional formatting with explicit targets",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Conditional formatting with explicit targets",
      "prompt": "A matrix displays variance in dollars. Business users want negative amounts to appear red and positive amounts green, while zero remains neutral. A colleague suggests sorting the matrix by variance value to encode the status visually. What is the appropriate report-level mechanism?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Sort descending and assume users infer negative values by their position."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Build a new many-to-many Status table related to all measures."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Apply conditional formatting rules or a field-value color measure using negative, zero and positive thresholds; verify accessibility beyond color alone."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove negative amounts in Power Query so no negative values require formatting."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-040",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-040",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-017",
      "topic": "Choosing paginated versus interactive report",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Choosing paginated versus interactive report",
      "prompt": "A regulator requires a monthly 120-page statement with repeated letterhead, fixed page breaks, page numbers, detailed tabular records, and precise printable PDF layout. Analysts still need interactive exploration elsewhere. Which Power BI artifact best fits the statement deliverable?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Create a paginated report for the controlled print layout, while keeping a separate interactive Power BI report for exploratory analysis."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a tooltip page and print it 120 times; hover layout controls page breaks."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create a single KPI card and export its underlying data; a card automatically includes repeated headers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Store the document as a DAX calculated column containing PDF binary contents."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-041",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-041",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-014",
      "topic": "Workspace roles and RLS enforcement",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Workspace roles and RLS enforcement",
      "prompt": "An HR semantic model uses an RLS role to restrict each region's employees. A user is added to that semantic model RLS role but is also granted workspace Contributor. In testing they can still browse unrestricted model data. What is the correct remediation?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Set the user to workspace Admin and keep the RLS membership; Admin receives stricter filters."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Grant report Build permission to all workspace members and assume Build enforces RLS on Contributors."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove explicit RLS roles and filter every report page using the region field."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Reduce the consumer to Viewer-level workspace/app permissions as appropriate, assign and test RLS roles, and separate edit responsibilities."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-042",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-042",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-017",
      "topic": "Overlapping RLS memberships",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Overlapping RLS memberships",
      "prompt": "A single report user belongs to RLS groups East and West. Each role filters Region dimension to one region. A security reviewer expects the user to see no rows because East and West are mutually exclusive. An audit must decide whether this entitlement design is safe. Which interpretation is right?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Role filters always intersect, so a user with multiple roles cannot see either region."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The user may see the union of East and West permissions; minimize overlapping memberships or use an audited single-role entitlement model."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "The user sees whichever region generated more FactSales rows on refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Membership in a second role cancels the first RLS role automatically."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-043",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-043",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-018",
      "topic": "Object-level security versus visual hiding",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Object-level security versus visual hiding",
      "prompt": "An analytics model exposes Salary and EmployeeName but department viewers may see only department totals. A developer hides Salary in the Fields list and removes it from report pages, then claims that users with model Build can no longer query the column. What control meets the requirement to withhold both Salary data and its metadata?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Hide the Salary field and rename it to a code; hidden fields are always inaccessible by DAX."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Place Salary only in a tooltip; tooltips cannot be accessed by any report viewer."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Define object-level security for the salary object in a suitable Viewer role, and test access to metadata and queries as that role."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use a DAX measure formatted as `;;;` to enforce model-level encryption of the salary column."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-044",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-044",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-025",
      "topic": "On-premises data gateway refresh failure",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "On-premises data gateway refresh failure",
      "prompt": "A published Import semantic model queries an on-premises SQL Server. Scheduled refresh worked yesterday but now fails after the service account password rotation. Reports still show old imported figures. Which service-side investigation should be performed first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Inspect semantic-model refresh history, gateway/connection status, and configured data-source credentials; update the failed authentication mapping."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Republish the unchanged PBIX repeatedly until refresh silently resumes."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set the report's page refresh to 1 second; this replaces Import semantic-model scheduled refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change every relationship to bidirectional so SQL Server trusts the gateway again."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V78-045",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V78-045",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-026",
      "topic": "App distribution and editor privileges",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "App distribution and editor privileges",
      "prompt": "A finance report must be distributed to 2,000 read-only employees. Only five developers may edit semantic models. A manager proposes giving every employee workspace Contributor to simplify sharing. What distribution approach better implements least privilege?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Give all recipients Contributor; a Power BI app is for developers only."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a public Publish to web embed link and treat it as authenticated finance access."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Give all employees semantic model ownership so they can open the report; RLS then automatically blocks edits."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Publish the report through a Power BI app or suitable viewer distribution with appropriate audience and licensing; reserve workspace edit roles for developers."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-018",
      "topic": "Separating report and semantic model version control",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Separating report and semantic model version control",
      "prompt": "A team uses Power BI Project (PBIP) format in Git. Two analysts edit report pages while another updates a DAX measure in the semantic model. The pull request includes localSettings.json and cache.abf as well as definition files. Which change best supports reviewable, reproducible version control?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Commit the binary cache.abf instead of semantic-model definitions because it is the authoritative source for merging measures."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Commit only the .pbip pointer file and discard both report and semantic-model folders after each pull request."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Convert the PBIP folder to screenshots of report visuals before merging so semantic changes are reviewed visually only."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Version the report and semantic-model definitions; ignore local cache and user settings."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V09-PBIP-019",
      "topic": "Merge conflict in report definition",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Merge conflict in report definition",
      "prompt": "Two branches of a PBIP repository edit the same report page definition and create a merge conflict. The team wants to retain both intended visual changes without losing one branch and without assuming a syntactically merged definition is semantically valid. What is the safest resolution?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Always choose the larger JSON file because it necessarily contains all visuals from both branches."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Delete the affected report definition and leave the .pbip pointer, which reconstructs the exact conflict-free report."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Resolve the conflicting report definitions deliberately, open the merged project in Power BI Desktop, validate both visuals and data bindings, and commit the tested result."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Suppress source-control diff review and republish the older PBIX because this guarantees both feature changes survive."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-003",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-030",
      "topic": "Testing dynamic security with a guest principal",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Testing dynamic security with a guest principal",
      "prompt": "A semantic model uses USERPRINCIPALNAME() to map users to permitted departments. An external B2B guest sees no rows while internal employees work. The model has a one-to-many relationship from Department to Sales. What is the strongest diagnostic before broadening access?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Make the guest a workspace Contributor so the RLS filter will be bypassed and assume the entitlement logic is fixed."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Match the actual guest principal to entitlements and test RLS as a Viewer."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change the Department-to-Sales relationship to many-to-many regardless of key uniqueness to permit more data paths."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove the RLS role and rely on a department slicer because that protects exported data for the guest."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-004",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-032",
      "topic": "Multiple entitlement rows with duplicate principals",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Multiple entitlement rows with duplicate principals",
      "prompt": "A regional model maps a sales manager to North and Central regions, and a salesperson to North only. The entitlement table holds one row per UserUPN–Region pair, but the manager appears twice. An author creates a 1:1 relationship from UserUPN directly to Sales and the model rejects it. Which redesign preserves the rights?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Represent user-region assignments in an entitlement bridge with tested security paths."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Remove one of the manager's regions to force the UserUPN column to become unique without business approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Join the entitlement table to fact sales by user name and declare both sides unique because the names look unique in the preview."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Apply a report page filter to North for all users and use editable bookmarks to expand the manager's view."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-005",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V25-RLS-034",
      "topic": "Secure bidirectional cross-filtering",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Secure bidirectional cross-filtering",
      "prompt": "An employee entitlement table filters Project, and Project filters Expense. A dynamic RLS role correctly limits Projects, but a report accessed through an alternate relationship reveals Expenses outside the permitted set. An analyst suggests setting every relationship to Both. Which action is justified?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Enable Both on every relationship because ordinary cross-filter direction is always equivalent to RLS filter propagation."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Add a hidden project slicer to the report so security follows all expense queries made through the semantic model."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Put USERELATIONSHIP into every measure, since inactive relationships automatically propagate RLS rules when measures are queried."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Audit the active security paths, use the Apply security filter in both directions setting only where supported and required, and test allowed and denied principals in the service."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-006",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V06-LIVE-004",
      "topic": "Extending a shared semantic model",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Extending a shared semantic model",
      "prompt": "A thin Power BI report is connected live to a centrally governed semantic model. The local team wants a new calculation, but is forbidden to alter the upstream model and also wants minimal duplicate datasets. What should the team check before claiming that a local model is required?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Duplicate the entire central semantic model into a separate Import model for every report author by default."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Edit measures in the remote semantic model without ownership because a live-connected report always has write access."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Check whether a report-level measure suffices before adding a supported local model."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a calculated column in the report canvas even when the live connection does not expose local table authoring."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-007",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V06-LIVE-007",
      "topic": "Model lifecycle and central measures",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Model lifecycle and central measures",
      "prompt": "Four product teams reuse the same certified semantic model but create different report pages. A core margin measure changes following an approved finance policy update. Leadership wants one governed definition with predictable downstream impact, rather than five manually reconciled measures. What architecture fits best?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Copy the margin DAX into every report and independently adjust it whenever teams ask for changes."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Maintain the approved margin measure centrally and validate dependent thin reports."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Replace the measure with a static PowerPoint label so every report appears to use the same metric description."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Disable semantic-model certification and let every report team use different financial definitions by default."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-008",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-002",
      "topic": "Reducing oversized import after folding is checked",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Reducing oversized import after folding is checked",
      "prompt": "A finance import has 60 million rows and 120 raw text attributes, but visuals use 8 numeric and key columns for the last 18 months. An engineer wants faster refresh and a smaller model while preserving the declared business grain. Which first transformation is most defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Filter out-of-scope historic rows and project unused columns in a foldable source step, then validate grain and query behavior before comparing model size and refresh time."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Duplicate every column into a calculated column so VertiPaq can compress both versions together."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Add a fully unique text index to each row and keep all attributes because high-cardinality strings always improve compression."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Merge all key dimensions into one 120-column fact and switch every relationship to bidirectional for refresh speed."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-009",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-003",
      "topic": "Numeric identifier with leading zeros",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Numeric identifier with leading zeros",
      "prompt": "A product export encodes SKU identifiers 00047 and 047 as distinct fixed-width text values, but Power Query automatically converted them to the numeric 47. The business states that these codes identify different catalog items and the source text remains available. Which correction preserves valid relationships?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Format the numeric 47 with one global number format so the two distinct identifiers can always be reconstructed."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep SKU numeric and set both relationship cross-filter directions to Both to recover the removed zeros."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove all duplicate numeric identifiers and invent random suffixes without a source-backed mapping."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Reimport the authoritative SKU as text and verify distinct values and relationships."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-010",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-010",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-004",
      "topic": "Incremental refresh late arriving changes",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Incremental refresh late arriving changes",
      "prompt": "A billing table is partitioned by InvoiceDate. Corrections to an invoice can arrive 45 days later but the current incremental-refresh policy refreshes only the most recent seven days. Finance reports stale values after successful refresh status. What adjustment directly addresses the described behavior?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Increase the number of years retained in the model without refreshing older partitions, since retention reruns every invoice row."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Turn off the InvoiceDate filter and let every partition fetch the entire source table for each refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Extend the refresh window to cover legitimate late corrections and validate the partition plan."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Set report page refresh to one second, which forces the Import dataset to reprocess historic source partitions."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-011",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-011",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-002",
      "topic": "DirectQuery source throttling",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DirectQuery source throttling",
      "prompt": "A DirectQuery report has five visuals that each issue expensive SQL whenever the user changes a date slicer. The source is rate limited and users experience query timeouts. A proposal is to add ten more visuals because that will parallelize and speed up each SQL query. What should be investigated first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add more visuals with equivalent queries because increasing concurrent queries always lowers the database cost."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Inspect source SQL and visual query costs, then tune interactions and source execution."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Disable query timeout settings permanently and assume no additional database capacity is needed."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Convert the slicer to a static image while keeping all cross-filters and SQL requests unchanged."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-012",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-012",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-004",
      "topic": "Hybrid storage mode selection",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Hybrid storage mode selection",
      "prompt": "A reporting system requires detailed recent transactions to appear within minutes while older transactions are queried primarily for month-end comparisons. Warehouse historical scans are expensive, and policy permits imported historic partitions. What design deserves evaluation?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Evaluate a hybrid or incremental-refresh design that keeps supported recent data accessible via DirectQuery and imports historical partitions, including capacity and feature prerequisites."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Import nothing and force every historic chart to execute a full detailed DirectQuery scan forever."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use only a static CSV snapshot from last year and label it as real-time for recent operational KPIs."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Disable transaction-date filters so all queries operate on all partitions equally regardless of freshness needs."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-013",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-013",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-005",
      "topic": "Data type mismatch across composite relationship",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Data type mismatch across composite relationship",
      "prompt": "An imported Customer dimension uses whole-number CustomerKey, but a DirectQuery orders source exposes a text key with values such as '0042'. Users report missing matches and one developer changes visual colors to hide the blanks. What is the appropriate modeling repair?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use conditional formatting to make blank groups invisible without investigating mismatched customer keys."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Turn on bidirectional filtering because it converts numeric and text keys into equivalent values automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Concatenate the unrelated Amount field to every customer key so Power BI can infer matching customers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Normalize the keys losslessly and test the cross-source limited relationship."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-014",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-014",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-002",
      "topic": "Avoid misleading truncated categorical axes",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Avoid misleading truncated categorical axes",
      "prompt": "An executive column chart compares five monthly revenues from 98 million to 102 million. Its vertical axis starts at 97 million, making a 4% change appear like a large multiple. The audience needs both the level and change without misleading visual scale. Which redesign is appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep the 97-million baseline but remove axis tick labels so viewers cannot notice the truncation."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Convert all revenues to the same color and omit values; a neutral palette makes any scale accurate."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use an honest scale for bar length and annotate the relatively small change."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Sort the months alphabetically rather than chronologically and call it a performance increase."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-015",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-015",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-003",
      "topic": "Drillthrough context multiple dimensions",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Drillthrough context multiple dimensions",
      "prompt": "A sales report shows Revenue by Region and Category. The analyst drills through from a selected Category bar and wants a detail page filtered to that category while still allowing Region to be selected separately. What should the author configure and test?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Hide Category from the original report because drillthrough automatically derives hidden fields from all sources."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Put Category in the drillthrough well of the detail page, ensure the detail visuals use related data, and verify passed filters and retained page-level controls."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use report page tooltip only because hover pages always support full detail navigation and slicer editing."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove the relationship between Product Category and Sales so no filters pass into the detail page."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-016",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-016",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-004",
      "topic": "Slicer synchronization without page clutter",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Slicer synchronization without page clutter",
      "prompt": "A report has Executive and Operations pages. The user chooses FiscalYear=2025 on Executive and expects the same year filter on Operations, but the Operations page must not show the year slicer. The two pages currently display different results. What should the author configure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Sync the year slicer across pages while hiding it on the Operations page."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Place two unrelated FiscalYear tables in the semantic model and let each report page choose its own independent year."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Hide the Operations slicer with a white fill but disable its synchronization with Executive."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace FiscalYear with a report title text box because text boxes propagate year filters through model relationships."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-017",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-017",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-005",
      "topic": "Field parameters display names and underlying measure",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Field parameters display names and underlying measure",
      "prompt": "A field parameter lets users switch a chart between Revenue, Units, and Gross Margin. A developer creates another disconnected text table and uses SELECTEDVALUE on its caption, but this changes the title while the visual still plots Revenue. What should the developer do to enable real field switching?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep Revenue on the chart and update only its displayed title to the selected parameter caption."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Add a page bookmark for each measure name without wiring the chart fields and assume DAX measure switching occurs."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change data type of Revenue to Text so SELECTEDVALUE can replace its values with measure definitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Bind the value well to a true field parameter containing the selectable measures."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-018",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-018",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-006",
      "topic": "Mobile layout composition",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Mobile layout composition",
      "prompt": "A desktop Power BI report has 12 visuals across a wide canvas. Phone users need three high-priority KPIs and one narrow table with accessible navigation, while the desktop layout should remain intact. Which design action is most appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Set every desktop visual width to 1 pixel so the same canvas will fit on a phone without separate layout."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Export a screenshot of the full desktop page, remove all keyboard interaction, and publish it as the mobile report."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create and test a phone-optimized mobile layout that prioritizes the relevant visuals, preserving desktop layout and verifying interactions on a small viewport."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Convert the model to DirectQuery because DirectQuery automatically rearranges report visuals into mobile columns."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-019",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-019",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V07-CHOICE-001",
      "topic": "Choose Import for disconnected mobile use",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Choose Import for disconnected mobile use",
      "prompt": "A sales team needs a Power BI report that remains fast when the cloud warehouse is briefly unreachable during the workday. Daily rather than second-by-second freshness is acceptable, but the full report must still use the last successfully loaded rows. Which storage mode is the better initial choice and why?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use DirectQuery because it permanently caches every source row after the first page view and never depends on source availability."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use Import and scheduled refresh because daily freshness and cached data are acceptable."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Choose a live connection solely because it guarantees that the external source can never become temporarily unavailable."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace the semantic model with hyperlinks to the source warehouse, since hyperlinks preserve slicer evaluation offline."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-020",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-020",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V07-CHOICE-002",
      "topic": "Plan source freshness with dimensional volume",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Plan source freshness with dimensional volume",
      "prompt": "A manufacturer has 15 million transactions per year and hourly small updates. The team claims every source should always use DirectQuery because Power BI Import cannot refresh more than once under any license. They need to meet an hourly SLA at a predictable cost. Which comparison is technically appropriate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Compare Import refresh, capacity, source latency, and DirectQuery workload against the SLA."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use DirectQuery without testing because it is always faster and always has lower load on the upstream database."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use Import without scheduling any refresh; merely opening a report forces all source rows to reload each hour."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Select storage mode from file-name extensions only, since csv files are always DirectQuery and SQL tables always Import."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-021",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-021",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V20-ONETOONE-002",
      "topic": "Duplicate key violates 1-to-1 assumptions",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Duplicate key violates 1-to-1 assumptions",
      "prompt": "EmployeeDirectory and EmployeeBenefit each supposedly have one row per EmployeeID. A refresh adds two EmployeeBenefit rows for employee 102 because two policies were active. A modeler insists on keeping the old 1:1 relationship without inspecting the changed business grain. Which response is defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep 1:1 because relationship cardinality is determined only by the first previewed 1,000 rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Set the employee key to text, which guarantees that all duplicate numeric IDs will vanish."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Drop one benefit policy arbitrarily at refresh so users will never see conflicting totals."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Reconfirm each table's true grain, identify whether multiple benefits are legitimate, and model an appropriate unique Employee dimension with a one-to-many benefit fact when required."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-022",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-022",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V20-ONETOONE-003",
      "topic": "Avoid gratuitous one-to-one fragmentation",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Avoid gratuitous one-to-one fragmentation",
      "prompt": "A customer table is arbitrarily split into CustomerName and CustomerMarketing by CustomerID, and both subsets hold the same one row per customer. Report authors frequently need columns from both; no different security or refresh lifecycle exists. Which design should be evaluated before keeping an extra 1:1 relationship?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add another many-to-many relationship between the two tables to make 1:1 design faster automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Duplicate CustomerMarketing into the Sales fact per invoice and use SUM on every attribute."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Consider merging same-grain customer attributes into one governed dimension."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a bidirectional relationship on every other model table so the customer split can be ignored."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-023",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-023",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V21-JUNK-002",
      "topic": "Combining low-cardinality transaction flags",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Combining low-cardinality transaction flags",
      "prompt": "An order-level fact contains three flags: IsGift, PaymentType, and FulfillmentChannel. Each flag is low-cardinality and used frequently together for slicing; they do not have independent descriptive attributes. The engineer wants a compact dimensional design without three separate mini dimensions. What option merits consideration?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Make each flag a separate measure only, because measures can always serve as relationship lookup keys."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Consider a junk dimension of observed valid flag combinations after cardinality profiling."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Append the flags as extra transaction rows so a single order produces three purchases in every report."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Delete the flags to reduce model size and tell users to infer fulfillment attributes from sales amounts."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-024",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-024",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V21-JUNK-003",
      "topic": "Flag combination explosion",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Flag combination explosion",
      "prompt": "An analyst proposes a junk dimension for 40 unrelated Boolean service flags in an event stream, reasoning that 40 flags always mean only 40 combinations. The complete set of possible combinations could be huge even though observed combinations are few. What must be evaluated before materializing the dimension?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Profile actual and possible combination cardinality before creating the flag dimension."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Generate every possible row for 40 flags because 2^40 equals only 40 distinct rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Store the flags as real-valued measures only, because dimensions do not support Boolean attributes."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Relate every flag table bidirectionally to the event fact even if the model becomes cyclic."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-025",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-025",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-007",
      "topic": "Accumulating snapshot vs transaction fact",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Accumulating snapshot vs transaction fact",
      "prompt": "A logistics process records each parcel's intake, dispatch, out-for-delivery, and delivery timestamps. One parcel should remain a single process instance while stage dates update. A designer proposes appending every stage transition as revenue transactions in the sales fact. Which fact design better answers parcel cycle-time questions?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Append each milestone date as a new revenue fact row because any new date represents additional sales."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep only the final DeliveryDate and discard other milestones because they cannot be related to the Date dimension."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a dimension with one row per second of process time and duplicate each parcel into all seconds elapsed."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use a parcel-grain accumulating snapshot with milestone dates for cycle-time measures."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-026",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-026",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V23-GRAIN-008",
      "topic": "Average shipment cost weighted by shipment",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Average shipment cost weighted by shipment",
      "prompt": "A shipping report has two carriers: A handled 100 deliveries at $5 each and B handled 2 deliveries at $100 each. A manager computes (5+100)/2=$52.50 and labels it average cost per delivery. What is the correct calculation at the requested delivery grain?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Average the two carrier unit costs without considering their delivery counts because every carrier has equal impact per delivery."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use MAX of carrier costs and divide by two because the most expensive carrier defines the sample."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Divide total carrier spending by total deliveries across both carriers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Add the two cost-per-delivery values and call the sum total expenditure across all deliveries."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-027",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-027",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-012",
      "topic": "Bridge prevents duplicate customer attribution",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Bridge prevents duplicate customer attribution",
      "prompt": "Customers can own several loyalty accounts, and each loyalty account can be shared by several customers. Purchases belong to AccountID. A report needs distinct purchased accounts by customer and a correct organization-wide grand total. What modeling and measurement caution is necessary?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Duplicate all account purchases to every customer and sum revenue across customers to calculate an unduplicated company total."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Model the customer-account bridge explicitly and use a distinct account measure; explain why customer subtotals need not add to the overall distinct account count."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Turn the bridge into a 1:1 relationship by keeping the first customer assigned to each shared account."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Multiply account revenue by the number of associated customers so every account is represented fairly in totals."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-028",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-028",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-013",
      "topic": "Sparse bridge and unknown entitlements",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Sparse bridge and unknown entitlements",
      "prompt": "A course model relates Student and Class through Enrollment. Several classes currently have no enrollments, but a catalog report must still show those classes. An analyst makes Enrollment the only driving table and uses inner joins everywhere. What should be changed in the report/model design?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep all classes in the Class dimension and report missing enrollments explicitly."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Delete unenrolled classes permanently because a factless enrollment bridge cannot represent zero enrollments."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create fake Enrollment rows for all classes with imaginary StudentIDs and count them as real students."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Replace every Class label with a total count measure, since dimension labels cannot be shown without bridge matches."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-029",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-029",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-003",
      "topic": "Calculated column vs measure under slicer context",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Calculated column vs measure under slicer context",
      "prompt": "A report author defines `NetPrice = Sales[Price] - Sales[Discount]` as a calculated column and then expects it to recalculate differently whenever a Region slicer changes the total. The source data is Import and discounts are row-level fixed amounts. What is the correct distinction?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Every calculated column recomputes its individual row value automatically on each slicer click before aggregation."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Slicers have no effect on measures and can affect only source-side SQL queries."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Creating a measure always requires converting the entire model to DirectQuery storage first."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Aggregate a processed row column or row iterator in a slicer-sensitive measure."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-030",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-030",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-005",
      "topic": "DAX blanks versus true zeros in KPI",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DAX blanks versus true zeros in KPI",
      "prompt": "A service KPI uses `DIVIDE([Resolved],[Created])` for a team that created zero tickets in the period. The product owner wants an explicit 'No activity' label and not a misleading 0% rate. What measure/visual behavior should be implemented?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Replace every zero denominator with one without disclosure and report a precise 0% rate as if it were observed."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use ordinary division and depend on the chart to ignore any division-by-zero error automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Keep a BLANK result for the undefined denominator and use a separately controlled label or conditional text for the no-activity state."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove the team from the semantic model so future periods cannot show its valid incidents."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-031",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-031",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-007",
      "topic": "DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK and missing identifiers",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK and missing identifiers",
      "prompt": "An imported Contacts table has ContactID values {9, 9, 12, BLANK}. Compliance asks for the count of unique known contact IDs only. An analyst uses DISTINCTCOUNT and gets a larger value than expected. Which DAX calculation directly answers the requirement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use COUNTROWS(Contacts) because repeated contact IDs always collapse to one row when counted."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK(Contacts[ContactID]) for known unique contacts."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use COUNTBLANK(Contacts[ContactID]) because it returns the number of nonblank distinct IDs."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use SUM(Contacts[ContactID]) because adding identifiers yields their unique population count."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-032",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-032",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-015",
      "topic": "CALCULATE replacement vs intersection",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "CALCULATE replacement vs intersection",
      "prompt": "A report page filters Product[Color] to Red. The author creates `[Blue Revenue] = CALCULATE([Revenue], Product[Color] = \"Blue\")` and another version wrapped in KEEPFILTERS. Both are displayed on the Red-only page. What difference should the reviewer expect?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use the default CALCULATE replacement rule versus a KEEPFILTERS intersection."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Both forms always calculate exactly the same all-color grand total because filters on Color are ignored entirely."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "KEEPFILTERS is only a report-page formatting option and cannot influence DAX filter context."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "CALCULATE permanently changes the Product table relationship cardinality on the semantic model."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-033",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-033",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-017",
      "topic": "Filter context on inactive date relationship",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Filter context on inactive date relationship",
      "prompt": "A model has one Calendar table, an active relationship to FactOrders[OrderDate], and an inactive relationship to FactOrders[DueDate]. A measure must show orders by DueDate in a matrix using Calendar[Month] without changing the default order-date behavior of other measures. What is the targeted DAX method?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Activate both date relationships simultaneously in the model and assume Power BI chooses a different one for each visual."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Change Calendar[Month] display color so the visual switches the physical date relationship automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Replace all FactOrders[OrderDate] values with DueDate so other measures continue to use original order dates."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use CALCULATE with USERELATIONSHIP on DueDate for this one measure."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-034",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-034",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V28-CONTEXT-019",
      "topic": "REMOVEFILTERS respecting date context",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "REMOVEFILTERS respecting date context",
      "prompt": "A matrix displays revenue by Product[Subcategory] for a selected year. Finance wants each row's percentage of all subcategory revenue in that same year, while keeping other slicers such as Region. Which denominator preserves the other filters?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use CALCULATE([Revenue], REMOVEFILTERS()) to remove every filter including Year and Region by definition."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use SUM(Product[Subcategory]) as the denominator because subcategory text automatically becomes revenue."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove only Product[Subcategory] filters from the denominator measure."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Calculate revenue per subcategory using a calculated column that never changes with date slicers."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-035",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-035",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V29-AGG-005",
      "topic": "Semi-additive inventory balance at month end",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Semi-additive inventory balance at month end",
      "prompt": "InventorySnapshot contains an end-of-day balance for every Store, Product, and Date. A store shows 10 units on day one, 12 on day two, and 9 on day three. Management requests the balance at the end of the three-day period, not cumulative units observed. What aggregation approach fits the metric?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "SUM every daily stock balance and label 31 units as the period closing inventory."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Evaluate the last applicable stock snapshot, not the sum across dates."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "AVERAGE stock balances and label the result as the exact balance at the last observed date."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "COUNTROWS of InventorySnapshot and treat each snapshot row as one currently held unit."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-036",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-036",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V29-AGG-006",
      "topic": "Avoid averaging averages across segments",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Avoid averaging averages across segments",
      "prompt": "Hospital A reported 200 appointments with an average wait of 5 minutes. Hospital B reported 2 appointments with an average wait of 50 minutes. A dashboard labels (5+50)/2 as average patient waiting time across the network. Which computation correctly weights the patient observations?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Calculate total waiting minutes divided by total appointments."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep (5+50)/2 because hospitals have equal contribution to every individual patient's wait."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use the longest wait from Hospital B as the network-wide mean to avoid understating outliers."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Add both averages together and divide by the number of months currently selected rather than appointments."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-037",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-037",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-003",
      "topic": "SUMX quantity-times-price",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "SUMX quantity-times-price",
      "prompt": "A transaction line table contains two rows: Quantity=3, UnitPrice=8 and Quantity=2, UnitPrice=15. Discounts are already incorporated into UnitPrice. An analyst needs an extended revenue measure, but the proposed expression is SUM(Quantity)*SUM(UnitPrice). What is the correct replacement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Multiply the total Quantity by total UnitPrice because aggregating before multiplying preserves the row-level association."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use DISTINCTCOUNT of Quantity plus DISTINCTCOUNT of UnitPrice to obtain a monetary total."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Average the two prices and ignore quantities because every line contributes the same amount of revenue."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use SUMX across lines to sum Quantity * UnitPrice at each transaction row."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-038",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-038",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-004",
      "topic": "Minimum row-level cost under multiple filters",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Minimum row-level cost under multiple filters",
      "prompt": "A table contains ShipmentID, Units, and CostPerUnit. Analysts want the cheapest total shipment cost for the currently selected routes, computed as Units×CostPerUnit per shipment. A colleague suggests MIN(CostPerUnit), which often chooses the wrong shipment. Which DAX pattern answers the requirement?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use MIN(CostPerUnit) alone because unit cost and total shipment cost always rank identically."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use SUMX over all shipments and label it as the minimum total for one shipment."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use MINX across filtered shipments for Units * CostPerUnit."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use COUNTROWS(Shipments) and assume the shipment with the fewest units is also least costly."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-039",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-039",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V32-CALCTBL-001",
      "topic": "Virtual table filter in CALCULATETABLE",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Virtual table filter in CALCULATETABLE",
      "prompt": "A model contains Products related 1:* to Sales. A DAX calculation needs the actual Sales rows for only those products whose Status is 'Active' while preserving the current month filter. The analyst proposes deleting inactive-product sales in Power Query. Which expression strategy better maintains reversible report context?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Delete Sales rows for inactive products from the stored source before every refresh, even for historical audit reports."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use CALCULATETABLE(Sales, Products[Status] = \"Active\") with the relationship."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change the Status column format to bold so the CALCULATETABLE filter is inferred automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use COUNTROWS(Products) as a replacement table, since scalar counts can be used wherever a row table is required."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-040",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-040",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V32-CALCTBL-005",
      "topic": "TOPN tie handling",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "TOPN tie handling",
      "prompt": "Finance asks for the top three products by Revenue. Two products tie for the third position, and an analyst assumes TOPN(3, ...) will always return exactly three rows. The finance report must document deterministic business tie policy. Which statement is technically correct?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "TOPN may return additional rows tied at the cutoff; specify an explicit tie-break policy and ordering or handle ties deliberately before publication."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "TOPN always returns exactly N rows regardless of tied values, so a fourth product proves data corruption."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Sorting a table by product color automatically converts tied revenue values into unequal revenue amounts."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove the Revenue measure and rank products by arbitrary product surrogate key without notifying the business."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-041",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-041",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-009",
      "topic": "Sensitivity label across exported file",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Sensitivity label across exported file",
      "prompt": "A government dashboard uses confidential procurement data. Recipients are allowed to view an interactive Power BI app but exported Excel files must retain a recognized classification. A developer proposes using a red report background as the security label. What control should be evaluated?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use red page wallpaper because a visible background enforces classification in every exported workbook."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Rename the report to SECRET and assume the semantic model and export files inherit enforced file encryption."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Hide the Export button on one page and assume all alternative download paths are blocked by the background color."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Evaluate a Purview sensitivity label and test supported export inheritance."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-042",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-042",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-011",
      "topic": "Paginated report parameters for print distribution",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Paginated report parameters for print distribution",
      "prompt": "An insurance regulator needs a printable 90-page monthly report with consistent repeating headers and one PDF per legal entity. Analysts also need each PDF to contain only the selected entity. Which Power BI deliverable should the team design?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a static tooltip on an interactive report page because tooltip rendering guarantees repeated page headers in PDF."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Create one Power BI KPI card and rely on its CSV export to contain the required 90 pages and footers."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a parameterized paginated report for controlled entity-specific PDFs."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Duplicate the entire model once per page and discard parameters because that is the only supported report filtering technique."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-043",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-043",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-015",
      "topic": "Least-privilege report viewers",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Least-privilege report viewers",
      "prompt": "A commercial team publishes a sensitive sales report to 3,000 employees, and only six BI maintainers may edit workspace content. One manager wants to add all consumers as workspace Member so they can open the app. The semantic model also defines region RLS. What permission plan should be tested?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Give every recipient Member rights because RLS is always stricter for workspace Members than for Viewers."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Keep maintainers in edit roles, distribute to supported app audiences or Viewer-level recipients, and test RLS per identity with licensing and access requirements."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Publish to web on a public URL and depend on an app title that says confidential to prevent unapproved access."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove all RLS roles and use a customer region slicer as the only restriction for report readers."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-044",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-044",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V22-ROLEPLAY-013",
      "topic": "Shipment business day vs order day",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Shipment business day vs order day",
      "prompt": "Orders contains OrderDate and DeliveredDate, and a shared Date table currently filters OrderDate through an active relationship. Operations needs one visual by DeliveredDate while other visuals continue to report order creation. The team suggests replacing every date relationship globally. What should be designed?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a DeliveredDate measure override or a separate role-playing date table."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace the active OrderDate relationship with DeliveredDate globally and assume existing order-date measures will remain unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create a calculated report title saying Delivered even though the data still aggregates by OrderDate."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Convert DeliveredDate to text and rely on chart sorting to determine which date column drives aggregation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V79-045",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V79-045",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V22-ROLEPLAY-014",
      "topic": "RLS coverage of duplicated date role",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "RLS coverage of duplicated date role",
      "prompt": "A fiscal-period restriction is implemented on DateOrder, while a modeler duplicates DateOrder into DateShip to support independent Order and Ship calendar slicers. They assume the original RLS rule automatically secures every duplicated dimension and downstream visual. Which validation is mandatory?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_PROVISIONAL",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Assume a copied table inherits every semantic-model RLS expression and always filters all related facts automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Turn DateShip into a report tooltip page because tooltips perform model-level security propagation."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Disable all relationships to the ShipDate role so unauthorized users cannot see anything related to shipments."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Test RLS paths for both DateOrder and DateShip; do not assume copy inheritance."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-001",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-001",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-005",
      "topic": "Anti-join for unmatched reconciliation",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Anti-join for unmatched reconciliation",
      "prompt": "Warehouse orders for the quarter have OrderID values O1, O2, O3, and O4. The archived settlement feed contains O1, O2, and O4, with no duplicate IDs in either feed. Finance needs the order rows absent from settlement, without dropping fields from the order table. Which Power Query join should be used?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a left anti join with Orders on the left and Settlements on the right; the result is O3."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a left outer join and keep all expanded rows as the unmatched set."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use an inner join on OrderID and filter out O3 afterward."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use a right anti join with Orders on the left; retain O3."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-002",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-002",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-006",
      "topic": "Local grouping requires contiguous keys",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Local grouping requires contiguous keys",
      "prompt": "A Power Query M transform groups records by CustomerID and an engineer proposes `GroupKind.Local` to reduce processing. The rows are ordered by TransactionDate, so the same customer appears in disjoint runs. The grouped total must combine every customer row into one group. What is the safest response?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Enable `GroupKind.Local` as is because it sorts by CustomerID implicitly."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Group normally, or sort by CustomerID before using `GroupKind.Local`."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Group by TransactionDate instead; grouping by time is always equivalent to customer grouping."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Buffer the table to force CustomerID runs to become adjacent."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-003",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-003",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-007",
      "topic": "Culture-safe date interpretation",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Culture-safe date interpretation",
      "prompt": "An English-locale workstation imports a supplier text field containing 03/04/2026, but the vendor confirms the format is day/month/year. Month-end auditing has already exposed incorrect March-versus-April postings. The source provides no separate ISO date and preserving the vendor meaning matters more than locale defaults. What transformation should be selected?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Accept the auto-detected date type because its locale always follows the supplier rather than the workstation."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace slash characters with dashes, leaving all other parsing defaults intact."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Convert the text using the declared vendor locale, and validate ambiguous and invalid dates with sample records."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Convert the date to a whole number before interpreting year, month, and day."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-004",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-004",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-008",
      "topic": "Column profiling limited to top rows",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Column profiling limited to top rows",
      "prompt": "A quality analyst opens Power Query Column Profile and sees no errors in a 2-million-row feed. Later a refresh fails on a malformed value near row 1.5 million. The analyst realizes profiling operated on a sample by default. What is the correct evidence-gathering action?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Conclude that every row is valid because the Column Profile graph has no red error segment."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Switch the report page to mobile layout, which updates the profiling scan to full source size."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Remove error rows without counting or locating them, then certify that the feed is complete."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Switch profiling to the entire dataset when feasible, and validate data types and errors with source-side checks."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-005",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-005",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V03-IMPORT-009",
      "topic": "Privacy firewall across confidential feeds",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Privacy firewall across confidential feeds",
      "prompt": "A payroll source is marked Private and a public web API accepts query parameters. A developer suggests sending payroll employee numbers to the public API to retrieve a description during mashup refresh; management has not authorized this information flow. What should the model author do before changing privacy options?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Preserve privacy isolation and review any disclosure of employee identifiers to the public API."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Ignore privacy levels to permit the mashup; query speed alone demonstrates that employee numbers are safe."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Mark payroll Public so all sources can receive employee numbers without disclosure assessment."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Publish the mashup as a paginated report because output format prevents confidential query parameters."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-006",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-006",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-006",
      "topic": "DirectQuery page visual workload",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DirectQuery page visual workload",
      "prompt": "A DirectQuery dashboard exposes 18 detail visuals and each change to one date slicer triggers many expensive source statements. Performance Analyzer shows long DAX-query duration, and database telemetry confirms scans against a large detail fact. Without changing business freshness requirements, which experiment is most defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add more identical detail visuals to parallelize the scans until each scan becomes faster."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Reduce unnecessary visual queries and test source indexing or aggregation strategies while measuring query duration."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Increase canvas width so the database can process larger SQL queries in one operation."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove Performance Analyzer and rely on anecdotal reports after changing color palettes."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-007",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-007",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-007",
      "topic": "Composite cross-source relationship mismatch",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Composite cross-source relationship mismatch",
      "prompt": "A semantic model relates imported Store[StoreCode] to DirectQuery Tickets[StoreCode] from a different source group. One source stores case-sensitive codes, and the other strips leading zeros; unexpected blank groups appear. What should be verified before changing cross-filter direction?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Turn on Both because bi-directional relationships always equate string values that differ in leading zeros."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a 100% stacked chart to make unmatched rows part of a known store category."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Reconcile key meanings and types losslessly; check limited cross-source relationship behavior."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Remove Store and count Tickets as unique stores without checking the original identifiers."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-008",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-008",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-009",
      "topic": "Refresh versus query for hybrid history",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Refresh versus query for hybrid history",
      "prompt": "A warehouse permits historical orders to be imported weekly but requires very recent events to appear within minutes. An engineer configures incremental refresh with the optional real-time DirectQuery partition, then claims every historical import partition is rerun for each report interaction. Which explanation is accurate?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Every visual interaction must reimport every historical row before issuing a DirectQuery statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Recent DirectQuery rows are permanently frozen at the time the last Desktop file was saved."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Incremental refresh stores only one CSV and never produces model partitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use imported historical partitions and a recent real-time DirectQuery partition."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-009",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-009",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V04-DQ-010",
      "topic": "DirectQuery source date filter type",
      "exam_objective": "Prepare the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "DirectQuery source date filter type",
      "prompt": "An operational DirectQuery report filters `EventTimestamp` to the last seven days but source SQL traces reveal expensive casts around the indexed timestamp column. The service owner needs reliable event windows with existing timezone rules preserved. Which approach should be investigated first?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Inspect native SQL timestamp predicates, timezone conversions, and database index usage."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Replace EventTimestamp with the formatted chart title text and rely on Power BI to infer date filtering."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Add an unrelated bidirectional Calendar relationship to force SQL to use the original timestamp index."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Disable all date filters so the indexed column is scanned without casting on every interaction."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-010",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-010",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-007",
      "topic": "Field parameter used for drillthrough context",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Field parameter used for drillthrough context",
      "prompt": "A product dashboard uses a field parameter to switch the axis between Category and Country. The author puts the parameter itself into the target drillthrough field well and finds that detail navigation fails. The business still wants both drill paths to work. What should be configured?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Turn the field parameter into an implicit aggregation of text and keep it as the sole drillthrough field."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Place the actual Category and Country fields as appropriate drillthrough fields, not the field parameter itself, then test each path."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a bookmark that discards the selected dimension and disables drillthrough on the page."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a calculated column with the parameter display name and assume it carries country/category entity identity."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-011",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-011",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-008",
      "topic": "Tooltips versus navigation for transaction details",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Tooltips versus navigation for transaction details",
      "prompt": "A compliance reviewer needs to open a full page containing a 50-row exception table and exportable identifiers after selecting an exception category. A developer proposes a report page tooltip that appears on hover and calls it equivalent to navigation. Which report feature better matches the requested workflow?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a static image tooltip of the exception table so users can select records in it."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Disable drillthrough and set the hover delay to zero to make tooltips equivalent to page navigation."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Configure category drillthrough to the exception details page and test context preservation."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Keep only the total-card value and explain that any category context automatically provides row-level evidence."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-012",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-012",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-009",
      "topic": "Visual calculation context when axis changes",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Visual calculation context when axis changes",
      "prompt": "A matrix uses a visual calculation to show a running total by Month under Year. A report author changes the layout to include Product within each Year and notices a new traversal order. They assume the old running total semantics remain identical regardless of axis and Reset configuration. What should they do?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Copy the visual calculation to a disconnected table because it always becomes an independent model measure."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Disable column subtotals; this guarantees that every running-sum reset occurs per product."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Replace Month with a tooltip field to keep the original matrix evaluation order unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Check the visual calculation traversal order and Reset scope after adding the Product axis."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-013",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-013",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V05-VS-010",
      "topic": "Accessibility and non-color information",
      "exam_objective": "Visualize and analyze the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Accessibility and non-color information",
      "prompt": "A safety dashboard uses red and green alone to indicate whether each site passes an incident-rate threshold. During keyboard-only testing, color-blind participants cannot identify the failing sites, and screen-reader descriptions do not mention status. Which redesign solves the primary communication problem?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Add explicit status labels or icons with readable accessible text, while preserving contrast and keyboard order."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use a more saturated red and green with no labels; stronger color alone guarantees accessibility."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Hide the site status from keyboard navigation because the numerical threshold remains visible elsewhere."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Increase animation speed on failed sites so screen readers can infer failure from motion."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-014",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-014",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-010",
      "topic": "Measure versus calculated column time grain",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Measure versus calculated column time grain",
      "prompt": "A membership model includes one row per contract with BaseFee and DiscountPct. The business wants discounted revenue recalculated under Date, Region and SalesRep slicers, with totals correctly summing each qualifying contract. Which calculation pattern best matches the request?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use `SUM(Contracts[BaseFee]) * (1-AVERAGE(Contracts[DiscountPct]))` for every combination."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Create a measure using `SUMX(Contracts, Contracts[BaseFee] * (1-Contracts[DiscountPct]))`."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Create one static text calculated column containing the grand total and display it for each region."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use `COUNTROWS(Contracts) * MAX(Contracts[BaseFee])` and ignore discounts."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-015",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-015",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-012",
      "topic": "Distinct count with missing IDs",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Distinct count with missing IDs",
      "prompt": "A customer event table includes CustomerID values 21, 21, 22, and BLANK. Audit wants the number of known unique customers, excluding all unknown IDs, without silently adding an unknown person. Which DAX measure is the direct fit?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use `DISTINCTCOUNT(Events[CustomerID])` and expect 2 because BLANK is always ignored."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use `COUNT(Events[CustomerID])` and expect two unique customers."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use `DISTINCTCOUNTNOBLANK(Events[CustomerID])` and expect 2 for this sample."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use `COUNTROWS(Events)` and treat 4 events as 4 distinct customer IDs."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-016",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-016",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-014",
      "topic": "SELECTEDVALUE fallback for multi-select",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "SELECTEDVALUE fallback for multi-select",
      "prompt": "A dynamic title should show the selected warehouse when exactly one is selected and otherwise display \"Multiple or none\". A slicer permits multi-select, and the title must avoid arbitrarily choosing the lowest Warehouse name. Which expression has the requested behavior?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use `MIN(Warehouse[Name])` because it detects whether there is exactly one warehouse."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use `COUNTROWS(Warehouse)` as the title text for both one and many selections."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use `FIRSTNONBLANK(Warehouse[Name], 1)` so multi-select always returns the declared fallback."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use `SELECTEDVALUE(Warehouse[Name], \"Multiple or none\")` in a title measure."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-017",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-017",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V27-DAXINTRO-016",
      "topic": "Row context and context transition",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Row context and context transition",
      "prompt": "A calculated customer-level expression loops through Customer rows and evaluates an aggregate over Sales, but it mistakenly returns the all-customer total for each customer because the plain aggregate is evaluated without filter context transition. What mechanism needs to be understood to correct the expression?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use a measure or `CALCULATE` for context transition through the relationship."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Wrap the aggregate in FORMAT to force it to use the current Customer row."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use a disconnected Customer table so Sales rows inherit the current Customer row automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Sort Customer alphabetically to make the SUM aggregate switch to row context."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-018",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-018",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V21-JUNK-004",
      "topic": "Missing combination in junk dimension",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Missing combination in junk dimension",
      "prompt": "A low-cardinality junk dimension was constructed only from combinations found in the first month of transactions. A new IsRush=Yes / Payment=Voucher combination appears in month two and the fact key now fails to map to a dimension row. What change best protects reporting correctness?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Filter out month-two transactions because a dimensional model should never admit new flag combinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Expand or derive the allowed attribute combinations from a governed source and ensure new fact combinations map before refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Change all affected facts to the first junk-dimension surrogate key regardless of flags."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Make the relationship bidirectional so the missing surrogate row is created during query evaluation."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-019",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-019",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V21-JUNK-005",
      "topic": "High cardinality flags avoid dimension explosion",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "High cardinality flags avoid dimension explosion",
      "prompt": "A telemetry table has 28 independent Boolean flags and a proposal would prebuild every possible flag combination as a dense junk dimension. Only several hundred combinations ever occur, and the flags are rarely filtered together. How should the design be evaluated?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Generate all 2^28 rows immediately because a large junk dimension is always more compressed than 28 low-cardinality columns."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Convert all flags into random surrogate numbers so users cannot filter any event by flag values."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Measure actual combination cardinality and query patterns; avoid materializing the entire theoretical cross-product."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Duplicate every event row once for each flag before loading the fact; duplicate fact grain is harmless."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-020",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-020",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V21-JUNK-007",
      "topic": "Transaction attributes versus descriptive entities",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Transaction attributes versus descriptive entities",
      "prompt": "A claims fact includes ClaimType and IsFraudSuspected, both low-cardinality descriptors. A proposed junk dimension also includes a free-text 400-character CustomerComment unique per claim. The team wants stable filters without ballooning model size. Which modification is strongest?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Treat every unique comment as another flag combination because comments are categorically identical to Boolean flags."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Remove ClaimType and IsFraudSuspected from the model to avoid ever having dimensions."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Duplicate the high-cardinality comment across all transaction lines to improve compression."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Keep low-cardinality flags in a small dimension, with unique comments stored separately."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-021",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-021",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-007",
      "topic": "Weighted defect rate across factories",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Weighted defect rate across factories",
      "prompt": "Two factories reported defect rates of 2% over 1,000 units and 10% over 10 units. An executive averages 2 and 10 to claim a 6% network defect rate. The model has Factory, ProducedUnits and DefectiveUnits and both facilities must be counted correctly. Which result is defensible?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Weight defects by produced units: 21 out of 1010, approximately 2.08% networkwide."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Report 6% because unweighted means of rates always give the correct company-wide rate."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Report 12% because defect percentages must be summed across plants."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use the highest plant rate of 10% for every unit to be conservative."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-022",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-022",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-008",
      "topic": "Weighted transaction discount rather than average",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Weighted transaction discount rather than average",
      "prompt": "A retailer sells 10 units at list price 100 with 20% discount, and one unit at list price 100 with no discount. An analyst averages the two discount percentages to get 10%, ignoring sold units. What is a better measure for the realized discount percentage by sales amount?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use `AVERAGE(Lines[DiscountPct])`, assuming each row always represents one currency unit."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Compute weighted discount as `1 - SUMX(Lines, Qty*UnitPrice*(1-DiscountPct)) / SUMX(Lines, Qty*UnitPrice)`."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use `MAX(Lines[DiscountPct])` as the realized aggregate discount percentage."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Use `SUM(Lines[DiscountPct])` and display it as a percentage of price."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-023",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-023",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-009",
      "topic": "Latest balance within selected period",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Latest balance within selected period",
      "prompt": "FactInventory holds one stock balance per Store, SKU, and SnapshotDate, with 9 units on Monday and 12 on Tuesday for the same Store/SKU. A visual displays the total on-hand stock for the week. The business wants the most recent valid snapshot, not cumulative stock-days. Which calculation principle applies?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Sum Monday and Tuesday rows to report 21 physical units on Tuesday."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Average all snapshots and present the result as exact latest stock units."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Find the last eligible snapshot date in context, then sum balances across Store/SKU at that date, accounting for missing snapshots."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Multiply the newest stock figure by the number of daily snapshots in the period."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-024",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-024",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V30-ITER-012",
      "topic": "Selected row maximum of extended cost",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Selected row maximum of extended cost",
      "prompt": "For the selected month, a FreightLines table holds Shipment S1 with two rows costing 40 and 20, and Shipment S2 with one row costing 50. A manager asks for the highest *total shipment cost*, rather than the highest individual line amount. Which operation yields the intended value?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use `MAX(FreightLines[LineCost])` and return 50."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Use `SUM(FreightLines[LineCost])` and return 110."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Use `COUNTROWS(FreightLines)` and return three as the monetary maximum."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Sum each ShipmentID before taking the maximum: 60."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-025",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-025",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-017",
      "topic": "RLS with workspace Contributor role",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "RLS with workspace Contributor role",
      "prompt": "A region manager must be restricted by a dynamic RLS role. The manager was granted Contributor in the report workspace to edit visuals and now sees data beyond the role predicate. The developer attempts to add the manager to two more RLS roles. What is the fundamental access problem?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Use Viewer permissions for RLS-restricted consumption and separately govern editing access."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Adding more RLS roles always intersects permitted rows and therefore fixes Contributor bypass."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Disabling the region slicer will enforce the hidden dynamic security predicate on Contributors."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Changing the visual from table to chart makes Contributor subject to RLS."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-026",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-026",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-020",
      "topic": "Scheduled refresh after gateway password rotation",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "Scheduled refresh after gateway password rotation",
      "prompt": "A weekly semantic-model refresh fails after the on-premises SQL account password rotates. The enterprise gateway is online, and an administrator confirms the SQL connector has outdated credentials. A report author wants to fix the error by increasing slicer cache duration. Which action addresses the failure?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Change report slicer sync settings because synced pages renegotiate SQL credentials."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Update the authorized gateway data source credentials and validate the refresh history with a test refresh."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Set all Power Query columns to text so password rotation no longer affects the data connection."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Change Power BI theme and republish the report pages without touching the gateway account."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-027",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-027",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V26-CAPSTONE-022",
      "topic": "RLS roles union rather than intersection",
      "exam_objective": "Manage and secure Power BI",
      "exam_subobjective": "RLS roles union rather than intersection",
      "prompt": "A Viewer belongs to two static RLS roles: North grants Store IDs {1,2}, and Online grants {2,3}. Each role uses a standard filter, and the team expects the combination to restrict the user to only Store 2. What result should their access test reveal?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Power BI always intersects separate RLS roles, so only Store 2 can appear."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The user must see no rows because any overlapping Store ID is treated as a security collision."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Multiple RLS roles generally combine granted rows by union, so the user may see {1,2,3}."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "The user must see only Store 1 because the alphabetically first role wins all conflicts."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-028",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-028",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-017",
      "topic": "Bridge customer-account totals",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Bridge customer-account totals",
      "prompt": "An Account fact contains Account A balance 80 and Account B balance 120. Customer X owns A and B, Customer Y jointly owns B. A bridge links customer-account memberships. The matrix shows X=200 and Y=120. Finance wants the actual institution total, not the sum of displayed customer subtotals. What should it show?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Sum the customer subtotals to show 320 because joint ownership creates extra financial assets."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Report 120 because the shared account is the only one involving more than one customer."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Return blank for every total since many-to-many models can never have valid aggregates."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Calculate distinct institution accounts in the total context: 200, not 320."
        }
      ],
      "gradingEnabled": false
    },
    {
      "draftId": "draft-pl300-v88-PL300-SUPP-V80-029",
      "sourceId": "PL300-SUPP-V80-029",
      "legacy_id": "PBI-V24-M2M-018",
      "topic": "Budget at category-year grain",
      "exam_objective": "Model the data",
      "exam_subobjective": "Budget at category-year grain",
      "prompt": "Actual Sales is recorded daily by SKU. Target holds one row per Category and FiscalYear, and managers want category-year comparisons without repeating each target for every SKU or month. One author directly links Targets by SKU, which does not exist in Target. Which modeling and measure principle is preferable?",
      "type": "single",
      "difficulty": "APPLIED_SCENARIO_AUTHOR_DRAFT_NOT_CALIBRATED",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Map conformed Category–Year targets and suppress SKU/Month values without an allocation policy."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Duplicate each category-year target onto every sale row and sum it to get annual target."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Build an arbitrary SKU key from the first three letters of Category because it creates an exact one-to-one mapping."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Assign an equal daily target to every day without defining allocation, then claim exact business accuracy."
